╭─ VULN-0011 ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│                                                                              │
│  Vulnerability Report                                                        │
│                                                                              │
│  Title: Client-certificate authentication is not enforced: handshake         │
│  completes without a client certificate and tls_client_verified() returns    │
│  true for an untrusted self-signed certificate                               │
│                                                                              │
│  Severity: HIGH                                                              │
│                                                                              │
│  CVSS Score: 7.5                                                             │
│                                                                              │
│  Target: /workspace/source                                                   │
│                                                                              │
│  Endpoint: tlse.c — TLS server handshake, Certificate handler                │
│  (tls_parse_payload case 0x0B)                                               │
│                                                                              │
│  Method: TLS handshake                                                       │
│                                                                              │
│  CVSS Vector: AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:H/I:N/A:N                            │
│                                                                              │
│  Description                                                                 │
│  A TLSe server that calls `tls_request_client_certificate()` still           │
│  completes the handshake with a peer that presents **no certificate at       │
│  all** — no verification callback runs, `tls_established()` returns 1, and   │
│  application data is exchanged — and no library API can make that handshake  │
│  fail. Additionally, when the application does not install a verification    │
│  callback (an explicitly optional parameter), a peer presenting **any        │
│  self-signed certificate it just generated** is accepted and                 │
│  `tls_client_verified()` returns **1**, because the client certificate is    │
│  never validated against a trust anchor. A server protecting resources with  │
│  mutual TLS through the library's documented client-certificate API is       │
│  therefore reachable by an attacker holding no credentials.                  │
│                                                                              │
│  Impact                                                                      │
│  Mutual-TLS protected resources are served to unauthenticated peers.         │
│  Demonstrated against an unmodified library server that requests a client    │
│  certificate and exposes a protected resource: (1) a client sending an       │
│  empty certificate list completes the handshake (`established=1`,            │
│  `client_verified=0`, zero certificates) and receives the protected data;    │
│  (2) a client presenting a freshly minted self-signed certificate obtains    │
│  `tls_client_verified()==1` and receives the protected data even when the    │
│  server gates on that accessor. Both TLS 1.2 and TLS 1.3 are affected. Any   │
│  server-side authorisation that trusts client-certificate authentication     │
│  (identity-bound access, service-to-service mTLS, device provisioning) is    │
│  bypassed, and because requests from the unauthenticated peer are accepted   │
│  as authenticated, modification on such endpoints follows the same path.     │
│                                                                              │
│  Technical Analysis                                                          │
│  **1. An empty certificate list is accepted as a successful handshake.**     │
│  `tls_parse_certificate()` treats an empty list as a successful parse and    │
│  returns a positive length:                                                  │
│                                                                              │
│  ```c title=tlse.c startLineNumber=8643 endLineNumber=8648                   │
│      CHECK_SIZE(3, buf_len, TLS_NEED_MORE_DATA)                              │
│      size_of_all_certificates = buf[0] * 0x10000 + buf[1] * 0x100 + buf[2];  │
│                                                                              │
│      if (size_of_all_certificates <= 4)                                      │
│          return 3 + size_of_all_certificates;                                │
│  ```                                                                         │
│                                                                              │
│  The server branch of the Certificate handler invokes the application        │
│  callback only when a certificate arrived (`&&                               │
│  context->client_certificates_count`), and explicitly permits the empty      │
│  list:                                                                       │
│                                                                              │
│  ```c title=tlse.c startLineNumber=9988 endLineNumber=9993                   │
│                          if (context->is_server) {                           │
│                              if ((certificate_verify) &&                     │
│  (context->client_certificates_count))                                       │
│                                  certificate_verify_alert =                  │
│  certificate_verify(context, context->client_certificates,                   │
│  context->client_certificates_count);                                        │
│                              /*  empty certificates are permitted for        │
│  client */                                                                   │
│                              if (payload_res == 0)                           │
│                                  payload_res = 1;                            │
│  ```                                                                         │
│                                                                              │
│  The TLS 1.0-1.2 branch has the same shape at `tlse.c:10003-10010`. The      │
│  handshake therefore runs to completion, `tls_established()` becomes 1, the  │
│  callback never fires, and the server delivers application data and accepts  │
│  requests from a peer that presented no identity. There is no API to         │
│  require a certificate — no equivalent of OpenSSL's                          │
│  `SSL_VERIFY_FAIL_IF_NO_PEER_CERT`; `tls_request_client_certificate()` only  │
│  sets `context->request_client_certificate = 1` (`tlse.c:12972-12978`),      │
│  used solely to emit the CertificateRequest. RFC 8446 §4.4.2 requires a      │
│  server that demands client authentication to abort with a fatal alert. The  │
│  library's own server example shows the bypassable pattern:                  │
│  `examples/tlshelloworld.c:156` requests a client certificate and `:179`     │
│  gates only on `tls_established(context) == 1`.                              │
│                                                                              │
│  **2. `tls_client_verified()` reports success for a certificate that was     │
│  never validated.** The accessor is:                                         │
│                                                                              │
│  ```c title=tlse.c startLineNumber=12980 endLineNumber=12984                 │
│  int tls_client_verified(struct TLSContext *context) {                       │
│      if ((!context) || (context->critical_error))                            │
│          return 0;                                                           │
│                                                                              │
│      return (context->client_verified == 1);                                 │
│  }                                                                           │
│  ```                                                                         │
│                                                                              │
│  `client_verified` is set to 1 only by `tls_parse_verify_tls13()`            │
│  (`tlse.c:9450-9451`) and `tls_parse_verify()` (`tlse.c:9525-9527`), after   │
│  the CertificateVerify signature checks out against the certificate the      │
│  peer itself supplied. Nothing on the handshake path validates that          │
│  certificate against a trust anchor: `tls_parse_certificate()` parses the    │
│  DER and stores the chain (`tlse.c:8749-8753`) with no issuer or chain       │
│  check, and the internal chain validators are never called from the          │
│  handshake — they run only if the application installs `tls_default_verify`  │
│  as its callback. A NULL callback is explicitly supported ("If the other     │
│  side just presented a certificate and certificate_verify is not NULL, it    │
│  will be called", `tlse.h:337-340`), so the default posture is fail-open     │
│  and `tls_client_verified()` returns 1 for a certificate the attacker        │
│  minted seconds earlier. Both defects reproduce on TLS 1.2 and TLS 1.3 (and  │
│  DTLS shares the same handler). The snapshot has one squashed commit ("Fix   │
│  for #164"), so no line history is available.                                │
│                                                                              │
│  PoC Description                                                             │
│  1. Generate an RSA server certificate/key pair and an attacker              │
