│  Remediation                                                                 │
│  Enforce client authentication instead of requesting it. In the server       │
│  branch of the Certificate handler, when the application asked for a client  │
│  certificate, abort the handshake with a fatal alert if the peer presented   │
│  an empty certificate list, in both the TLS 1.3 and TLS 1.0-1.2 paths, as    │
│  RFC 8446 section 4.4.2 requires. Make the default fail closed when a        │
│  client certificate was requested but no verification callback was           │
│  supplied, so a peer cannot be reported as verified against a certificate    │
│  the library never validated; applications that intentionally accept         │
│  arbitrary certificates can still supply an explicit callback. Document      │
│  that `tls_client_verified()` reflects proof of possession of the presented  │
│  key rather than validation against a trust anchor, and correct              │
│  `examples/tlshelloworld.c` so it gates on both `tls_established()` and      │
│  `tls_client_verified()` and performs real chain validation in its           │
│  `verify_signature` callback instead of returning `no_error`                 │
│  unconditionally.                                                            │
│                                                                              │
